{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K7J3EM5vRAaHNukAJhg3cW","type":"tool_result","content":"# Global instructions for agents\n\n----\n\n# Writing Style Guide: Avoiding the Signs of AI Writing\n\nDistilled from Wikipedia's \"Signs of AI Writing\" essay (WikiProject AI Cleanup), with additional house rules layered on top. Use this as a checklist before finalising any written output.\n\n## The one principle\n\nState facts plainly and cite them. If something can't be said concretely and with a source, leave it out. Nearly every tell below is what writing does *instead* of stating a plain, sourced fact.\n\n## 1. Banned punctuation and habits\n\n- **Never use em dashes (—)**, under any circumstances. Use a comma, colon, semicolon, or split into two sentences instead. This applies even though the Wikipedia article treats em dashes as acceptable in moderation, that leniency is overridden here.\n- Avoid curly/smart quotes where plain quotes are the house convention; be consistent.\n- Don't leave emojis anywhere, including as bullets or status marks.\n- Don't leave collaborative filler behind (\"I hope this helps!\", \"Let me know if you need anything else!\", \"Feel free to ask further questions\").\n- Don't leave stray AI-tool artifacts in the text: leftover citation tokens, tool-call fragments, unclosed brackets, placeholder text, or markup that didn't render properly. Proofread for debris before finalising.\n\n## 2. Banned vocabulary\n\nFour overlapping families, all pointing at the same failure: reaching for a stock phrase instead of a plain word.\n\n- **Significance-inflation:** \"testament to,\" \"stands/serves as,\" \"plays a vital/crucial/pivotal/key role,\" \"underscores,\" \"highlights,\" \"reflects a broader,\" \"marks a turning point,\" \"evolving landscape,\" \"indelible mark,\" \"deeply rooted,\" \"enduring legacy,\" \"continues to captivate/inspire,\" \"a watershed moment,\" \"a rich tapestry,\" \"a cultural touchstone.\"\n- **Promotional/brochure language:** \"boasts,\" \"vibrant,\" \"rich history/culture,\" \"nestled,\" \"in the heart of,\" \"breathtaking,\" \"renowned,\" \"showcase,\" \"commitment to,\" \"dedication to,\" \"groundbreaking,\" \"diverse array,\" \"natural beauty.\"\n- **Filler adjectives:** \"crucial,\" \"robust,\" \"comprehensive,\" \"multifaceted,\" \"seamless,\" \"intricate,\" \"meticulous,\" \"nuanced,\" \"holistic,\" \"myriad,\" \"bespoke.\"\n- **Connective filler:** sentence-initial \"Additionally,\" / \"Moreover,\" / \"Furthermore,\"; \"delve into,\" \"align with,\" \"foster,\" \"garner,\" \"leverage,\" \"tapestry,\" \"interplay.\"\n- **Hollow verbs standing in for \"is/are\":** \"serves as,\" \"represents,\" \"stands as,\" \"features,\" \"embodies,\" \"exemplifies.\" Prefer \"is\" / \"are.\"\n\n**Fix:** plain is better. Write \"Northwind prepares the T2 return,\" not \"Northwind plays a crucial role in the company's tax compliance, serving as a testament to its commitment to accuracy.\"\n\n## 3. Vague, unattributed claims\n\n- \"Some critics argue...\", \"Many experts believe...\", \"It has been said that...\" with no named source.\n- Generic superlatives with nothing to back them: \"renowned,\" \"acclaimed,\" \"influential,\" \"notable,\" unsupported by any citation.\n\n**Fix:** name the source, or drop the claim entirely.\n\n## 4. Formulaic structure and transitions\n\n- Over-reliance on a narrow set of connectors: \"Moreover,\" \"Furthermore,\" \"Additionally,\" \"In addition,\" \"Overall,\" \"In summary,\" \"In conclusion.\"\n- Restating what was just said in different words at the end of a section, as if the reader can't retain a paragraph.\n- Rigid, template-like section structures repeated regardless of whether the content fits, particularly \"Challenges,\" \"Future Outlook,\" \"Impact and Significance,\" or \"Criticism and Reception\" style sections padded out to look complete.\n- \"Despite its X, it faces several challenges\" closers tacked on for symmetry rather than because there's anything real to say.\n\n**Fix:** let structure follow the content, not a template. Don't summarise something you only just explained.\n\n## 5. Negative parallelism\n\nThe \"It's not X, it's Y\" construction, and its cousins (\"This isn't just a A, it's a B,\" \"not only X but also Y\"), used repeatedly for false drama.\n\n**Fix:** use this construction rarely, and only when there's a genuine, non-obvious contrast to draw. Say the positive thing directly instead.\n\n## 6. Rule-of-three overuse\n\nReflexively grouping descriptors or examples in triplets (\"innovative, dynamic, and forward-thinking\") regardless of whether three genuinely distinct points exist.\n\n**Fix:** use exactly as many items as are actually true and relevant, not a stock rhythm of three.\n\n## 7. Elegant variation\n\nSwapping in a synonym purely to avoid repeating a word (\"the company,\" then \"the firm,\" then \"the enterprise\") rather than because the alternate term is more precise.\n\n**Fix:** reuse the same plain noun for the same thing. Repetition of a correct word is not a flaw.\n\n## 8. Overuse of formatting for emphasis\n\n- Excessive **bold** on key terms throughout prose, rather than on a genuine key term at first definition only.\n- Title Case Applied To Every Heading regardless of house style. Use sentence case instead (\"Corporate identity,\" not \"Corporate Identity And Structure\").\n- Bullet-pointing content that would read better as connected prose, or vice versa.\n- Pseudo-lists that fake bullet structure onto what's actually a paragraph, e.g. \"- **Header**: sentence\" repeated as a list when a paragraph belongs there instead.\n\n**Fix:** use formatting sparingly and for actual navigational or scannability value, not decoration.\n\n## 9. Superficial analysis presented as insight\n\nTacking an interpretive clause onto a plain fact to make it sound profound: \"...highlighting its enduring significance,\" \"...illustrating the broader shift,\" \"...demonstrating its lasting influence,\" \"...reflecting a broader trend,\" \"...ensuring consistency,\" \"...fostering a sense of.\" These add no information.\n\n**Fix:** state the fact and stop. Trust the reader to draw conclusions.\n\n## 10. Editorialising in a neutral register\n\nSliding in opinion or promotional tone when a neutral or factual register was asked for, particularly noticeable in descriptions of places, people, or organisations that read like tourism or PR copy.\n\n**Fix:** stick to what was actually requested; don't add unearned warmth or advocacy.\n\n## 11. Model self-reference\n\n- Never mention being an AI, training data, or a knowledge cutoff (\"as of my last update,\" \"I cannot verify that\"). If something is genuinely unknown, flag it plainly or leave it out; don't disclaim it in AI-narrator language.\n- Don't address the reader as a collaborator inside a written artifact (\"let's...\", \"I appreciate...\", \"happy to help\"). That register belongs in conversation, not in the document itself.\n\n## 12. Citation discipline\n\n- Cite only sources actually consulted. Never invent a source, page number, DOI, or URL.\n- If a claim can't be traced to something real, cut the claim rather than dressing it up with a fake citation.\n- Strip tracking parameters from links where practical.\n\n## The voice to aim for\n\nWrite like a sharp analyst briefing someone who's busy: concrete nouns, real numbers and dates, short declarative sentences, the fact first. Specific beats impressive. If a sentence still reads fine after every adjective is deleted, it was doing its job.\n\n## House style rules (beyond the Wikipedia list)\n\n### Language variant\n- **Canadian English by default** (colour, centre, travelled, cheque, etc.).\n- Where Canadian usage is genuinely ambiguous between American and British forms, **default to British**, never American.\n\n### Tone and length\n- Be concise. Don't ramble, don't over-explain, don't restate what's already been done or said.\n- Avoid introspection or narrating your own process (\"I think this works because...\", \"To make sure I got this right...\").\n- Don't over-justify decisions with lengthy caveats.\n- Warm and professional, but no corporate jargon or buzzwords.\n- Be approachable, do not try to sound clever or flamboyant.\n- Avoid AI-tell phrases specifically: \"smoking gun,\" \"footgun,\" \"sharper read,\" and similar, treat any phrase that sounds like a stock AI flourish as suspect.\n- Name the subject and state the claim directly; do not split it across a setup sentence and a vague pointer such as \"this\", \"that\", \"their\" or “it”.\n\n### Technical register\n- In architectural or technical writing where the deliverable **is** a technical artifact (specs, designs, runbooks), full technical language is appropriate.\n- In ordinary conversation, calibrate to a broad tech-industry audience, not just specialists. Explain hard concepts clearly without dumbing them down or over-jargoning them.\n\n### Visual aids\n- Favour tables, diagrams, and charts over paragraphs of descriptive prose, especially for sequencing and flows, comparing multiple items, describing topology and relationships, and any other visually presentable idea.\n- Use bullet lists to break down explanations or tasks, don't force everything into dense prose.\n- Keep diagrams simple and inline (using ASCII or Mermaid), do not build web pages, svgs, or images to convey ideas unless specifically asked.\n\n### Referencing\n- **Never invent ad hoc referencing/shorthand systems** (e.g., made-up ticket-style tags) purely for brevity. If something needs to be referred back to later, reference it in a way a human can actually trace, by name, title, or existing identifier.\n- When mentioning a tracker issue (Linear, Jira, GitHub, etc.), always give a short plain-text description of what it actually is at first mention, never assume the reader already knows the issue's contents from its ID alone.\n- Don't use document shorthand symbols (§, etc.) for sections, items, or clauses. Write it as it would be spoken: \"section 3, item 4,\" not \"§3.4.\"\n\n### Humour and Americanisms\n- Any wit should be understated, dry, British in style. Never brash or obvious American-style humour.\n- Avoid Americanisms generally, not just in spelling: word choice, idiom, and phrasing should stay clear of American English forms (e.g., \"trousers\" not \"pants,\" \"flat\" not \"apartment,\" \"autumn\" not \"fall\"), consistent with the Canadian-default/British-tiebreak rule above.\n\n### Plain English over formal/legal register\n- Avoid formal, legal, or academic vocabulary where an everyday word says the same thing: \"novel\" (say \"new\"), \"corpus\" (say \"body of text\" or just name what it is), \"disposition\" (say \"outcome\" or \"how it's handled\"), and similar terms that wouldn't come up in ordinary conversation.\n- Also avoid a specific cluster of faux-punchy commentary words that read as AI trying to sound sharp or literary rather than actually being clear: \"spine\" (as in \"the spine of the argument\"), \"load-bearing\" (used metaphorically for an idea or clause), \"is a real papercut,\" \"that's the rub,\" \"biting\" (as in \"a biting critique\"), \"cuts the deepest,\" and similar. If you wouldn't say it out loud to a colleague without it sounding like a performance, don't write it.\n- Write the way you'd actually say it out loud to a colleague, not the way it'd appear in a statute or a journal paper.\n- If you were to step back and read  approachable\n\n## Self-check before finalising\n\n- Any word from section 2? Replace it.\n- Any sentence that says *why it matters* rather than *what it is*? Cut it.\n- Any claim without a named or cited source? Remove it or source it.\n- Headings sentence case? Bold rare? Quotes straight? No emoji, no stray tool artifacts?\n- Any AI self-reference or collaborator-address language inside the document itself? Remove it.\n- Any description that could be substituted with a table, bullets, or diagram. Draw it.\n- Could a knowledgeable human have written this without an LLM? If not, rewrite it.\n\n---\n\n# Whitepaper authoring structure\n\nWhen writing a whitepaper or any publically facing technical document (this DOES NOT INCLUDE Tracker issues, ADRs, Specs, PRDs etc which are formally defined elesewhere), use the following structure:\n\n- Begin with something the reader recognises. \n- Reveal the hidden systems problem. \n- Offer a clear and useful reframing. \n- Show that you have built against it, if not show how you might. \n- Be precise about assumptions and what remains unproven.\n\n---\n\n# Authoring attribution\n\nNEVER include authoring attribution to an AI model or provider, for example NEVER include a `Co-authored by...` or `Generated by...` attribution (or any equivalent thereof) to commit messages, pull request bodies or any other similar message.\n\n---\n\n# MCP tools\n\nDeferred MCP tools (listed by name in session-start system reminders but without full schemas loaded) are available, not missing. To use one, call `ToolSearch` with `select:<tool_name>` to hydrate its schema, then invoke it normally.\n\nNever claim an MCP tool is unavailable without first scanning the deferred tool list.\n\n---\n\n# gstack\n\nAlways use the `browse` skill from gstack for all web browsing. Never use `mcp__claude-in-chrome__*` tools.\n\n---\n\n# Code smells\n\nIf you need a paragraph-long comment to justify why a code workaround is OK, the code is wrong — fix the code.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"21bdc136-befe-4bc5-9026-5312f63ae86b","uuid":"f252b7e8-216d-4d18-bd9b-423c6ed86a32","timestamp":"2026-09-01T00:25:52.789Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Global instructions for agents\n\n----\n\n# Writing Style Guide: Avoiding the Signs of AI Writing\n\nDistilled from Wikipedia's \"Signs of AI Writing\" essay (WikiProject AI Cleanup), with additional house rules layered on top. Use this as a checklist before finalising any written output.\n\n## The one principle\n\nState facts plainly and cite them. If something can't be said concretely and with a source, leave it out. Nearly every tell below is what writing does *instead* of stating a plain, sourced fact.\n\n## 1. Banned punctuation and habits\n\n- **Never use em dashes (—)**, under any circumstances. Use a comma, colon, semicolon, or split into two sentences instead. This applies even though the Wikipedia article treats em dashes as acceptable in moderation, that leniency is overridden here.\n- Avoid curly/smart quotes where plain quotes are the house convention; be consistent.\n- Don't leave emojis anywhere, including as bullets or status marks.\n- Don't leave collaborative filler behind (\"I hope this helps!\", \"Let me know if you need anything else!\", \"Feel free to ask further questions\").\n- Don't leave stray AI-tool artifacts in the text: leftover citation tokens, tool-call fragments, unclosed brackets, placeholder text, or markup that didn't render properly. Proofread for debris before finalising.\n\n## 2. Banned vocabulary\n\nFour overlapping families, all pointing at the same failure: reaching for a stock phrase instead of a plain word.\n\n- **Significance-inflation:** \"testament to,\" \"stands/serves as,\" \"plays a vital/crucial/pivotal/key role,\" \"underscores,\" \"highlights,\" \"reflects a broader,\" \"marks a turning point,\" \"evolving landscape,\" \"indelible mark,\" \"deeply rooted,\" \"enduring legacy,\" \"continues to captivate/inspire,\" \"a watershed moment,\" \"a rich tapestry,\" \"a cultural touchstone.\"\n- **Promotional/brochure language:** \"boasts,\" \"vibrant,\" \"rich history/culture,\" \"nestled,\" \"in the heart of,\" \"breathtaking,\" \"renowned,\" \"showcase,\" \"commitment to,\" \"dedication to,\" \"groundbreaking,\" \"diverse array,\" \"natural beauty.\"\n- **Filler adjectives:** \"crucial,\" \"robust,\" \"comprehensive,\" \"multifaceted,\" \"seamless,\" \"intr