{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CeboFtsTYc9EhEdEY497W","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ByorkXoBavqpcBm39UhNtx","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/shftwst/.claude/skills/faffter-dark-nlspec/SKILL.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":929,"cache_read_input_tokens":83371,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":929,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XY5XLTrTJcSFTSLGnTsGZB","session_id":"21bdc136-befe-4bc5-9026-5312f63ae86b","uuid":"c97b841a-0923-4350-b38e-036ca3149098","timestamp":"2026-09-01T00:48:01.253Z","request_id":"req_011CeboFt1NKNUnqBkUWhwNj","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Prep E1 skeleton + compose spec"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ByorkXoBavqpcBm39UhNtx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: faffter-dark-nlspec\n3\tdescription: \"Full nlspec-format spec producer for the `spec` slot — the heavy, formal counterpart to the lite default. Runs via faff-prep, not the user `/` menu.\"\n4\tuser-invocable: false\n5\tjudgement_seam: confidence, marker, specqual\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# faffter-dark-nlspec\n9\t\n10\tFull nlspec-format spec generation, usable as a delegated `spec` skill in faff-prep.\n11\t\n12\tThe nlspec format is inspired by [NLSpec-Spec](https://github.com/TG-Techie/NLSpec-Spec) by TG-Techie. No code from that project is used.\n13\t\n14\tConfigure in `.faffrc`:\n15\t\n16\t```yaml\n17\tslots:\n18\t  spec: faffter-dark-nlspec\n19\t```\n20\t\n21\tWhen invoked, faff-prep passes the issue context, explore findings, and the spec contract (the gateway _Spec readiness (fixed)_ section). This skill produces the spec body; faff-prep handles attachment, validation, and lifecycle.\n22\t\n23\t## Input\n24\t\n25\tFaff-prep provides:\n26\t\n27\t- Issue title, description, acceptance criteria, labels, dependencies\n28\t- Explore findings (codebase state, architecture, relevant files)\n29\t- The spec contract from the gateway Spec-readiness section (canonical markers: `**Chosen:**`, `**Punt:**`, `**Assumes:**`)\n30\t- The writing style rules (skimmable, no invented labelling schemes)\n31\t\n32\t## Output\n33\t\n34\tA single markdown document following the full nlspec four-phase arc. Faff-prep reads the output, validates markers, and attaches it to the issue.\n35\t\n36\t## Spec Format\n37\t\n38\t### Preamble\n39\t\n40\tOpening paragraph naming the artifact, the issue it addresses, and the intended audience (the build agent, human reviewers).\n41\t\n42\t### 1. WHY — Problem and Principles\n43\t\n44\t**Open with the load-bearing model.** The WHY's first paragraph states the one idea the reader needs to understand the rest — the mechanism the spec turns on, in plain terms — before problem detail. (Rendering hoists this if buried; the producer should write it deliberately, not leave it for normalise to find.)\n45\t\n46\t**Problem statement:** status quo → pain → what this change does about it. Three sentences max.\n47\t\n48\t**Design principles:** bold-lead paragraphs for any non-obvious constraints that should govern implementation decisions. Only include principles that would cause you to reject an otherwise-valid implementation. If there are none, omit the section.\n49\t\n50\t**Reference context:** if the implementation touches or mirrors existing systems, a brief table:\n51\t\n52\t| System | Language | Relevance |\n53\t|---|---|---|\n54\t| `src/auth/middleware.ts` | TypeScript | Existing auth layer this extends |\n55\t\n56\t**Scope statement:** one sentence on where this fits in the broader system. Not a sales pitch — a locator.\n57\t\n58\t### 2. OUT OF SCOPE\n59\t\n60\tFor each excluded item:\n61\t\n62\t- **Name** — what's excluded\n63\t- **Why excluded** — one sentence\n64\t- **Extension point** — where a future issue would add this (file, module, interface)\n65\t\n66\tThis section prevents scope creep during build AND tells the next agent where to extend. Every item the explore step surfaced as \"related but not this issue\" belongs here.\n67\t\n68\t### 3. WHAT — Vocabulary, Types, and Interfaces\n69\t\n70\t**Vocabulary:** if the spec introduces domain terms, define them before first use. A two-column table (term → definition) is sufficient.\n71\t\n72\t**Type definitions:** use pseudocode RECORD/ENUM/INTERFACE notation. Group related fields, annotate constraints, mark mutability.\n73\t\n74\t```\n75\tRECORD UserSession:\n76\t  id: UUID                    # assigned on creation, immutable\n77\t  user_id: UUID               # FK to users table\n78\t  expires_at: Timestamp       # must be > now at creation\n79\t  scopes: Set<Scope>          # non-empty; validated against user.allowed_scopes\n80\t\n81\t  CONSTRAINT expires_at > created_at\n82\t```\n83\t\n84\t**API surfaces / component props / data schemas:** whatever the build agent needs to know about the shape of things. Use the type notation above where precision matters, prose where it doesn't.\n85\t\n86\t**Design decisions:** every tradeoff table, \"X vs Y\" comparison, or architecture pick must conclude with a canonical marker per the spec contract:\n87\t- `**Chosen:** X` / `**Decision:** X` — closed\n88\t- `**Punt:** X or Y — needs human` — open, in \"Open Questions\" section. When the punt's class is clear, emit the optional `(decides: <owner>)` suffix per the gateway (`product | architecture | qa | security | any`, or a free-form handle); omit when ambiguous, never guess. The `faff-contract:spec-readiness` block is unchanged — a tagged punt still emits `{ \"marker\": \"punt\" }`.\n89\t- `**Assumes:** X exists` — external dependency, in \"Assumptions\" section\n90\t\n91\t### 4. HOW — Behavior\n92\t\n93\t**Architecture and approach:** how the pieces connect. Prose for the overview, pseudocode for the mechanics.\n94\t\n95\t**Pseudocode at ambiguity points:** anywhere prose alone could be interpreted two ways, provide a labeled-steps block:\n96\t\n97\t```\n98\tPROCEDURE handle_expired_session(session):\n99\t  1. Check session.expires_at against current time\n100\t  2. IF expired:\n101\t     a. Revoke all active tokens for session.user_id\n102\t     b. Emit SessionExpired event with { session_id, user_id, expired_at }\n103\t     c. Return 401 with body { error: \"session_expired\", retry: false }\n104\t  3. IF not expired:\n105\t     a. Extend session.expires_at by SESSION_TTL\n106\t     b. Continue to next middleware\n107\t```\n108\t\n109\t**Behavior summaries:** before complex procedures, a one-sentence plain-English summary of what the procedure accomplishes and why.\n110\t\n111\t**Edge cases and error handling:**\n112\t- Fallback chains with explicit precedence\n113\t- Boundary conditions in pseudocode (not prose)\n114\t- Error categories: which are retryable, which are terminal, what the caller sees\n115\t\n116\t**Failure modes — how the approach falls over, and how you'd notice.** Distinct from edge cases (inputs the code must handle): these are ways the chosen approach could be wrong, and the observable that reveals it. For each non-obvious risk in the design:\n117\t\n118\t- **The failure** — what could be wrong about the approach, not the code: a confound that invalidates a measurement, an assumption that silently doesn't hold, a benefit that may not exist.\n119\t- **How you'd know** — the concrete signal: the metric that wouldn't move, the test that would fail, the number that comes back null.\n120\t- **What it means** — proceed / narrow / abandon. A null or negative result is a valid outcome to name here, not a gap to hide.\n121\t\n122\tEmit only above the complexity bar (same judgement as Scenarios): a spike, a novel integration, or any design whose value rests on an unvalidated assumption. A mechanical CRUD change has none — forcing the section there is bloat.\n123\t\n124\t**Anti-patterns:** near the section they affect, document what NOT to do and why. Format: `**Anti-pattern:** X. Why: Y.`\n125\t\n126\t### 5. SCENARIOS — born-verifiable main objectives\n127\t\n128\tA dedicated `## Scenarios` section, after HOW, expressing the spec's **main objectives** as scenarios so what the work must achieve is born verifiable — not just prose the verifier has to re-derive.\n129\t\n130\t**Coverage — proportionate, not always-on.** Emit a scenario only for a main objective **above the complexity bar**: a non-trivial behavioural objective with a non-obvious observable outcome. Trivial objectives get none — a scenario for them is bloat, and the house skimmability rule outranks blanket consistency. The producer judges the bar.\n131\t\n132\t**Behavioural objectives → Given-When-Then:**\n133\t\n134\t```\n135\tGiven <precondition>\n136\tWhen <the change/action>\n137\tThen <the observable, testable outcome>\n138\t```\n139\t\n140\t**Non-functional objectives → assertions/constraints**, not forced Given-When-Then. A constraint like \"no PII in logs\", \"10k rps sustained\", or \"p99 < 200ms\" is an assertion line — two complementary forms, behaviour vs non-functional, not one format jammed onto both.\n141\t\n142\tThis is the same language as holdout BDD; a holdout is just the withheld subset of these scenarios (one language, two visibilities).\n143\t\n144\t**Anti-pattern:** restating the DONE checklist as Given-When-Then. Why: DONE already mirrors the body; scenarios sharpen the WHAT's main objectives, they do not duplicate DONE. The dedicated section + complexity bar exist to avoid exactly this.\n145\t\n146\t**Marking a holdout.** Optionally withhold a scenario from the builder and reserve it for the code-blind evaluator — mark it in place, in this same section, never a second section or format:\n147\t\n148\t- **Fenced GWT block:** open the fence with the info string `holdout` (```` ```holdout ```` or `~~~holdout`) instead of a bare fence — every criterion unit inside is a holdout.\n149\t- **Assertion bullet:** prefix the bullet `holdout:` (case-insensitive) — `- holdout: The p99 latency MUST be < 200ms`. The prefix is stripped before classification; it is never part of the stored criterion text.\n150\t\n151\t**Selection rules.** Marking is optional — zero holdouts is fully valid. Mark a minority (guideline one-in-three, never more than half, and never all — the builder must retain at least one visible scenario). A holdout must verify behaviour the WHAT/HOW body already requires, never be the sole statement of a requirement — prefer a different concrete instantiation of a stated rule (other values, another path) over introducing new behaviour only a holdout states. DONE items are never marked.\n152\t\n153\t### 6. DESIGN DECISION RATIONALE\n154\t\n155\tCollect all decisions made throughout the spec. For each:\n156\t\n157\t- **Bold question:** the decision that was faced\n158\t- **Options considered** with brief pros/cons\n159\t- **Marker:** `**Chosen:** X — rationale`\n160\t\n161\tRejected alternatives are documented here so the build agent doesn't re-propose them and future readers understand why.\n162\t\n163\tTemporal anchors where relevant: \"At the time of writing, library X does not support Y\" — so the decision can be revisited when the landscape changes.\n164\t\n165\t### 7. OPEN QUESTIONS AND ASSUMPTIONS\n166\t\n167\t**Open Questions:** all `**Punt:**` items collected in one place. Each with enough context that a human reviewer can make the call without re-reading the full spec.\n168\t\n169\t**Assumptions:** all `**Assumes:**` items collected in one place. Each with a validation instruction (how the build agent checks whether the assumption holds before starting).\n170\t\n171\t### 8. DONE — Definition of Done\n172\t\n173\tA testable checklist that **mirrors the body sections 1:1**. The closed-loop rule:\n174\t\n175\t- Every WHY/WHAT/HOW section that introduces a requirement gets a matching DONE item\n176\t- Missing DONE items reveal untestable requirements — the spec has a gap\n177\t- Orphaned DONE items reveal ungrounded requirements — the body has a gap\n178\t\n179\tFormat:\n180\t\n181\t```markdown\n182\t### From WHY\n183\t- [ ] Problem statement's pain point is addressed (specific observable behaviour)\n184\t\n185\t### From WHAT (types and interfaces)\n186\t- [ ] UserSession record matches the defined schema\n187\t- [ ] scopes validated against user.allowed_scopes on creation\n188\t\n189\t### From HOW (behaviour)\n190\t- [ ] Expired sessions return 401 with { error: \"session_expired\", retry: false }\n191\t- [ ] SessionExpired event emitted with correct payload\n192\t- [ ] Non-expired sessions extended by SESSION_TTL\n193\t\n194\t### From HOW (edge cases)\n195\t- [ ] Concurrent expiry + renewal race condition handled per pseudocode\n196\t```\n197\t\n198\tEach item must be concrete enough to write a test against. \"Works correctly\" is not a DONE item. \"Returns 401 with body `{ error: \"session_expired\" }`\" is.\n199\t\n200\t**Eval coverage.** If the work introduces or changes an LLM-judgement seam, a DONE item registers its grader `KIND` + ≥1 eval case + the seam-registry row in this same ticket — all autonomous-doable. Recording/accepting the baseline value is a separate human-supervised step and is never required by this DONE item.\n201\t\n202\t**Integration smoke test:** pseudocode for a single end-to-end path that exercises the happy case. This is not exhaustive — it's the \"if this one thing works, the plumbing is connected\" test.\n203\t\n204\t### 9. APPENDICES (optional)\n205\t\n206\tLettered A, B, C. For essential content that would disrupt narrative flow in the body:\n207\t- Attribute catalogs\n208\t- Format references\n209\t- Error catalogs with codes and messages\n210\t- Usage examples\n211\t\n212\tOnly include appendices when the body would become unreadable without extracting the content. Most specs won't need them.\n213\t\n214\t## Confidence self-rating\n215\t\n216\tEnd the output with a confidence line on its own:\n217\t\n218\t```\n219\tconfidence: high | medium | low\n220\t```\n221\t\n222\t- **high** — every decision is marked, no open questions remain, DONE mirrors body completely\n223\t- **medium** — some `**Punt:**` items exist but are non-blocking, or the explore findings were ambiguous in places\n224\t- **low** — significant unknowns, architectural uncertainty, or the issue may need splitting\n225\t\n226\tThis line is consumed by faff-prep for its gate decision (autonomous mode: `high` → promote; `medium` → attach + flag for review; `low` → park) and is **retained on the attached spec** — downstream consumers (faff-graft, faff-beep-boop, and faff-tidy's spec-health pass) read it as durable provenance and a re-spec signal; a retained `confidence: medium` maps to the `needs-decision-first` routing verdict. It is both a signal to the caller and a lasting property of the spec.\n227\t\n228\t## Quality bar — self-review before returning\n229\t\n230\tThis producer discharges the `spec` slot's quality bar by inheritance, not by defining a second one: before emitting the spec and confidence line, it runs the sibling `faffter-noon-spec/SKILL.md` → _Self-review before returning_ section in full, exactly as written there.\n231\t\n232\t- **What's inherited.** The reviewer brief, the `blocker`/`major`/`minor` severities, the acting-on-findings rules, the return-the-review requirement, and the narrowing-only-refresh exemption all carry over unchanged — this section adds nothing to that checklist and drops nothing from it.\n233\t- **In-context clause.** This skill only ever runs as a dispatched producer subagent, never at the gateway's top level, so the inherited review always takes its in-context form here: a fresh-reasoning pass performed inside this same subagent against the drafted spec and the codebase, rather than a further clean-context subagent dispatch.\n234\t- Nesting therefore stays single-level by construction for this producer — the top-level case the inherited section also allows for simply never arises, so there is nothing to choose between at dispatch time; the review's purpose (every claim checked against the codebase before the spec is trusted) is unaffected by the different form.\n235\t- **Rating cap.** The inherited downgrade rule binds this producer exactly as written: landing on ≥1 `blocker` finding, or ≥3 `major` findings, forecloses a `high` self-rating regardless of how settled the draft felt going in.\n236\t- Cap the self-rating at `medium` when that threshold is hit, and read the confidence self-rating section immediately above as subject to this cap rather than as an independent judgement call.\n237\t\n238\t## Contract artifact\n239\t\n240\tAfter the prose spec and the `confidence:` line, append **one** fenced code block — tagged `faff-contract:spec-readiness`, as the **last** thing in the output — declaring the markers you just wrote, so faff-prep (the consumer) parses them **deterministically** (no LLM re-read of your prose) and pipes them to `faff contract spec-readiness`. You authored the markers and the confidence token, so you declare them directly; the block mirrors the prose, it is not a second source of truth.\n241\t\n242\t````\n243\t```faff-contract:spec-readiness\n244\t{ \"confidence\": \"<your confidence token: high|medium|low>\",\n245\t  \"decisions\": [ { \"marker\": \"chosen\" | \"punt\" | \"assumes\" | \"none\" }, ... one per non-trivial decision section, in document order ] }\n246\t```\n247\t````\n248\t\n249\t- **One** block, at the very end. `decisions` lists each non-trivial decision section's canonical marker in order (`chosen` / `punt` / `assumes`; `none` only if a multi-option section is missing one — a clean spec has none).\n250\t- Do **not** include `provenance_present` — faff-prep computes that from the provenance stamp it adds at attach time.\n251\t- The block is machine-only (a human reader can ignore it). **Always emit it** — it is the deterministic path; a present-but-malformed block fails loud downstream (producer breakage), so emit valid JSON matching the shape exactly. (Omitting it falls back to faff-prep reading your prose — the absent-block fallback.)\n252\t\n253\t## Rules\n254\t\n255\t- The spec contract markers (`**Chosen:**`, `**Punt:**`, `**Assumes:**`) are mandatory — this skill adds structure around them, not instead of them.\n256\t- The writing style rules from faff-prep (skimmable, no invented labelling schemes, restate subjects) apply fully.\n257\t- Pseudocode is language-agnostic. Do not write in a specific programming language — the build agent translates to the project's language.\n258\t- The spec must be buildable by a coding agent with only the spec as context. If a section requires external knowledge not in the explore findings, mark it `**Assumes:**`.\n259\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XY5XLTrTJcSFTSLGnTsGZB","session_id":"21bdc136-befe-4bc5-9026-5312f63ae86b","uuid":"681d3a51-21e0-4b07-a071-e7633d0c6112","timestamp":"2026-09-01T00:48:01.260Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Prep E1 skeleton + compose spec"}
