{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a572b2d4abe0d7ffb","tool_use_id":"toolu_01XqTDGk7NCqbki4NjQ15ayY","description":"Writing .faff/specs/gk-20260901-vesiuq.md","subagent_type":"general-purpose","usage":{"total_tokens":101577,"tool_uses":16,"duration_ms":310307},"last_tool_name":"Write","uuid":"ff9c9ed1-ec60-4027-8407-9ca6fb570dae","session_id":"21bdc136-befe-4bc5-9026-5312f63ae86b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011Cec8gF3HJwwzPGhyca2RL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013nVHxkK3NnNHDw6xjNBbvN","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/shftwst/workspace/shftwst/faff-sut-p1-link-shortener-l4-3/.faff/specs/gk-20260901-vesiuq.md","content":"# E2 — Schema migration and persistence layer: the `links` table and its store methods\n\n> Spec: faffter-dark-nlspec · 2026-09-01 · autonomous · claude-code/unknown · confidence: high · spec-review: pending.\n> build-tier: pending\n\nThis is the build spec for epic E2 of the link-shortener service (gitkey `gk-20260901-vesiuq`), written for the build agent and human reviewers. E2 extends the E1 skeleton (gitkey `gk-20260901-r99do5`, shipped and on main): it adds the first real golang-migrate migration, which creates the `links` table, and the persistence-layer methods that insert a link and fetch a link by code, wired into the existing `internal/store` package. E2 inherits E1's fixed stack (Go 1.22 stdlib `net/http`, Postgres 16, pgx v5, golang-migrate v4) unchanged; it decides no new architecture and stands up no new runnable surface. It stops short of the `POST /shorten` and `GET /{code}` HTTP endpoints (E3/E4) and of TTL expiry enforcement (E5).\n\n## 1. WHY — problem and principles\n\nThe load-bearing idea: E1 shipped the migration *runner* against an empty `migrations/` directory, so the schema is still empty and the store package exposes no queries. E2 is the first slice that puts real data structure behind the running service: one migration file that creates the `links` table, and two store methods (insert, fetch-by-code) that read and write rows in it. After E2, the code→url mapping has somewhere to live, which is the precondition every later product endpoint depends on.\n\nProblem statement: the running service has a datastore but no schema and no persistence code, so nothing can be stored or retrieved. E2 adds the `links` table via a versioned migration applied at startup and the store methods to insert and read a link. This resolves E1's deferred point that `schema_migrations` bookkeeping was not yet populated (E1 ran the runner against an empty dir on purpose).\n\nDesign principles:\n\n- **Extend E1, do not re-architect it.** The stack, the migration runner (`internal/migrate`), the connection foundation (`internal/store` `Open`), and the DSN-scrubbing error discipline are fixed by E1. E2 adds a migration file and store methods inside those seams; it changes no existing interface and introduces no new dependency (golang-migrate and pgx are already in `go.mod`).\n- **Idempotency comes from golang-migrate's version bookkeeping, not defensive DDL.** The migration is a plain `CREATE TABLE`, not `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`. Applied-once-ness is guaranteed by the `schema_migrations` version row golang-migrate maintains; restart re-runs the runner, which finds nothing pending (`ErrNoChange`) and makes no change. Defensive `IF NOT EXISTS` would mask a genuine dirty-state or double-apply bug rather than expose it.\n- **The store never leaks the DSN, matching E1.** E1 established that any error which could embed the connection string is scrubbed before logging. E2's query-path errors are wrapped and returned to the caller; they carry the failing SQL and Postgres error, never the DSN, so the query path needs no new scrubbing, but it must never wrap the DSN into an error either.\n- **Persist the `expires_at` column now; enforce nothing on it.** The `expires_at` column is part of E2's schema because the table shape is decided here, but TTL semantics (computing it from `ttl_seconds`, expiry-on-read, expired-code 404) are epic E5. E2 stores whatever `expires_at` a caller supplies (in practice `NULL` until E5) and reads it back unchanged; it applies no expiry logic.\n\nReference context:\n\n| System | Language | Relevance |\n|---|---|---|\n| `internal/store/store.go` | Go | E1 connection foundation (`Open` → `*pgxpool.Pool`, `SafeReason`). E2 adds the `Links` repository and `Link` record here. |\n| `internal/migrate/migrate.go` | Go | E1 migration runner (`Run(dsn, dir)`). Unchanged by E2; it now finds one real migration to apply instead of an empty dir. |\n| `migrations/` | SQL | E1 ships only `.gitkeep`. E2 adds the first `NNNNNN_*.up.sql` / `.down.sql` pair here. The Dockerfile already copies this dir to `/migrations`. |\n| `cmd/api/main.go` | Go | E1 startup sequence. Calls `migrate.Run` then serves. Unchanged by E2. |\n| `docker-compose.yml` | YAML | E1 topology (api + Postgres 16). Unchanged by E2; the born-verifiable checks run against this composed stack. |\n\nScope statement: E2 is the persistence layer of Project P1, sitting between E1 (the runnable skeleton) and E3 (`POST /shorten`, which writes through E2's `Insert`) / E4 (`GET /{code}`, which reads through E2's `ByCode`).\n\n## 2. OUT OF SCOPE\n\n- **`POST /shorten` mint endpoint** — validating a URL, minting a 7-char base62 code, calling `Insert`. Why excluded: epic E3. Extension point: a new handler on `internal/httpapi`'s router that constructs a `Link` and calls `Links.Insert`.\n- **`GET /{code}` redirect and 404** — resolving a code to a URL, issuing a 302, mapping the not-found sentinel to 404. Why excluded: epic E4. Extension point: a wildcard `GET /{code}` handler that calls `Links.ByCode` and maps `ErrLinkNotFound` to a 404.\n- **TTL expiry semantics** — deriving `expires_at` from a `ttl_seconds` input, treating an expired row as absent, returning 404 for an expired code. Why excluded: epic E5. Extension point: E5 computes `expires_at` at mint time and adds an expiry predicate to the read path (`ByCode`, or a new method); E2 only stores and returns the column.\n- **Code generation / uniqueness policy** — deciding how a code is minted or how collisions are retried. Why excluded: epic E3 owns minting. E2's `Insert` surfaces a duplicate-key condition as a typed error so E3 can retry, but it neither generates codes nor decides the retry policy. Extension point: E3's mint loop consumes `ErrDuplicateCode`.\n- **Wiring the repository into an HTTP handler or `main.go`** — constructing `Links` in `main` and passing it to the router. Why excluded: no endpoint consumes it until E3, so wiring it now would be dead code. The repository is exercised by its own integration test against the composed Postgres. Extension point: E3 constructs `NewLinks(pool)` and hands it to the router alongside the existing pool.\n- **Secondary indexes on `links`** — e.g. an index on `expires_at` for an expiry sweep. Why excluded: no E2 query filters on `expires_at`; the primary-key lookup on `code` needs no extra index. Extension point: a later migration under `migrations/` if E5/E6 add an expiry query.\n\n## 3. WHAT — vocabulary, types, and interfaces\n\nVocabulary:\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| link | A stored mapping from a short `code` to a destination `url`, with a creation timestamp and an optional expiry timestamp. |\n| code | The short identifier that is the primary key of the `links` table. Stored as text; E2 presumes no particular length or alphabet (E3 mints it). |\n| repository | The `Links` type in `internal/store` that owns the SQL for inserting and fetching a link. |\n| `schema_migrations` | The bookkeeping table golang-migrate maintains to record the applied migration version and whether the last migration left the schema dirty. |\n\n### Schema — the `links` table\n\nThe migration creates exactly this table. Column types are Postgres types.\n\n```\nTABLE links:\n  code        TEXT         PRIMARY KEY          # the short code; natural key, lookups are by code\n  url         TEXT         NOT NULL             # destination URL, stored verbatim; no length cap\n  created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ  NOT NULL DEFAULT now()   # row creation time, DB-assigned\n  expires_at  TIMESTAMPTZ  NULL                 # optional expiry; NULL means no expiry. E2 stores, E5 enforces.\n```\n\n- No surrogate key: `code` is the primary key, so a duplicate code insert fails with a unique-violation the store maps to `ErrDuplicateCode`.\n- No `CHECK`, no foreign keys, no secondary indexes in E2.\n\n### Migration files\n\nTwo files under `migrations/`, a golang-migrate versioned pair (6-digit sequential prefix, the golang-migrate default the E1 runner already reads via the file source):\n\n```\nmigrations/000001_create_links.up.sql     # CREATE TABLE links ( ... )   -- plain CREATE, not IF NOT EXISTS\nmigrations/000001_create_links.down.sql   # DROP TABLE IF EXISTS links\n```\n\nThe `up` file creates the table exactly as the schema above. The `down` file drops it. E2 never runs `down` automatically; the pair exists because golang-migrate expects both directions and a reviewer needs the reverse to be correct.\n\n### Types — the store surface (added to `internal/store`)\n\n```\nRECORD Link:\n  Code:       string        # maps to links.code; required, non-empty\n  URL:        string        # maps to links.url; required, non-empty\n  CreatedAt:  Timestamp     # maps to links.created_at; DB-assigned on insert, populated on read\n  ExpiresAt:  Timestamp?    # maps to links.expires_at; nil ⇔ SQL NULL\n\nREPOSITORY Links:\n  # Constructed from the E1 pgxpool. Holds the pool; owns the links SQL.\n  NewLinks(pool: *pgxpool.Pool) -> *Links\n\n  # Insert persists one link row. code is the primary key.\n  #   - On success: returns nil. created_at is DB-assigned (DEFAULT now()).\n  #   - On a duplicate code (unique_violation, SQLSTATE 23505): returns ErrDuplicateCode.\n  #   - On any other DB error: returns a wrapped, DSN-free error.\n  Insert(ctx: Context, link: Link) -> error\n\n  # ByCode fetches one link by its code.\n  #   - On a hit: returns the fully-populated Link, nil.\n  #   - On no row: returns zero Link, ErrLinkNotFound.\n  #   - On any other DB error: returns zero Link, a wrapped DSN-free error.\n  ByCode(ctx: Context, code: string) -> (Link, error)\n\nSENTINELS:\n  ErrLinkNotFound    # ByCode found no row; E4 maps this to a 404\n  ErrDuplicateCode   # Insert hit a duplicate primary key; E3 maps this to a mint retry\n```\n\n**Insert and `created_at` / `expires_at`.** `Insert` writes `code`, `url`, and `expires_at` and lets the DB default supply `created_at`. It does not read `link.CreatedAt` on the way in. `expires_at` is bound as `NULL` when `link.ExpiresAt` is nil and as the timestamp otherwise.\n\n**Design decision — repository shape vs package funcs.** Options: (a) a `Links` struct holding the pool with methods; (b) package-level functions taking a `*pgxpool.Pool` on each call. E1's own OUT OF SCOPE names \"a `Links` repository in `internal/store`\", and a struct gives E3/E4 a single value to hold and pass to handlers. **Chosen:** a `Links` repository struct constructed by `NewLinks(pool)`. Package funcs would force every future handler to thread the pool through each call.\n\n**Design decision — `code` column type.** Options: `TEXT`, or `CHAR(7)` / `VARCHAR(7)` to match the eventual 7-char code. **Chosen:** `TEXT PRIMARY KEY`. E3 owns the 7-char base62 rule; baking length into the schema here would couple E2 to an E3 decision and force a migration if E3's format ever changed. `TEXT` in Postgres has no performance penalty over `VARCHAR`.\n\n## 4. HOW — behaviour\n\n**Architecture and approach.** E2 touches two seams, both already built by E1:\n\n1. **Migration.** Dropping the `000001_create_links.{up,down}.sql` pair into `migrations/` is the entire startup-side change. `cmd/api/main.go` already calls `migrate.Run(cfg.DatabaseURL, \"migrations\")` at boot; `internal/migrate` already applies pending `.sql` files via golang-migrate's file source and treats `ErrNoChange` as success. With one real file present, the empty-dir short-circuit in `migrate.Run` no longer fires (the dir now contains a `.sql` file), so the runner calls `m.Up()`, which applies version 1 and writes the `schema_migrations` row. No Go code in `main.go` or `internal/migrate` changes.\n\n2. **Store methods.** `internal/store` gains the `Link` record, the `Links` repository, `NewLinks`, `Insert`, `ByCode`, and the two sentinels, using the same pgx v5 pool E1's `Open` already returns. `store.go`'s existing `Open`/`SafeReason` are unchanged.\n\n**Insert behaviour.**\n\n```\nPROCEDURE Insert(ctx, link):\n  1. Execute:\n       INSERT INTO links (code, url, expires_at) VALUES ($1, $2, $3)\n     binding $3 = NULL when link.ExpiresAt is nil, else the timestamp.\n     (created_at is omitted so the column DEFAULT now() supplies it.)\n  2. IF the DB reports unique_violation (SQLSTATE 23505):\n       return ErrDuplicateCode\n  3. IF any other DB error:\n       return a wrapped error whose text carries the DB error, never the DSN\n  4. ELSE return nil\n```\n\n**ByCode behaviour.**\n\n```\nPROCEDURE ByCode(ctx, code):\n  1. Execute:\n       SELECT code, url, created_at, expires_at FROM links WHERE code = $1\n  2. IF no row (pgx.ErrNoRows):\n       return zero Link, ErrLinkNotFound\n  3. IF any other DB error:\n       return zero Link, a wrapped DSN-free error\n  4. ELSE scan into Link (expires_at → nil when SQL NULL) and return it, nil\n```\n\n**Migration applied-once / restart idempotency.**\n\n```\nPROCEDURE startup_migration (unchanged E1 runner, new file present):\n  1. migrate.Run sees migrations/ is non-empty (a .sql file exists) → skips the empty-dir short-circuit\n  2. Constructs the golang-migrate runner and calls m.Up()\n  3. First boot: version 1 pending → CREATE TABLE links runs; schema_migrations = (version 1, dirty false); status \"applied\"\n  4. Later boot: version 1 already applied → m.Up() returns ErrNoChange → status \"no change\"; no DDL runs, table unchanged\n```\n\nIdempotency on restart is therefore a property of golang-migrate's `schema_migrations` version row, not of the DDL. The `CREATE TABLE` is plain (no `IF NOT EXISTS`): if it ever ran twice against the same schema it would error loudly rather than silently succeed, which is the behaviour that would surface a bookkeeping bug.\n\n**Edge cases and error handling.**\n\n- **Duplicate code on insert** → `ErrDuplicateCode` (mapped from SQLSTATE 23505). Terminal for E2; E3 decides whether to retry with a fresh code. `Insert` distinguishes it from other errors so the caller need not parse pgx error text.\n- **Unknown code on fetch** → `ErrLinkNotFound`. Terminal, expected, not an error condition to log at error level. E4 maps it to 404.\n- **`expires_at` round-trip** → a `NULL` `expires_at` reads back as a nil `ExpiresAt`; a non-nil timestamp reads back equal (to the DB's timestamptz precision). E2 applies no comparison against `now()`.\n- **DB unreachable mid-query** → the pgx call returns a connection error; `Insert`/`ByCode` wrap and return it. It reaches the caller, not the logs directly, and carries no DSN (the DSN lives only in the pool config, not in query-path errors).\n\n**Failure modes.**\n\n- **The failure:** the migration lands the runner in a *dirty* state (a `CREATE TABLE` that partially fails, or a crash mid-apply), after which golang-migrate refuses further migrations until the version is forced clean. **How you'd know:** `SELECT dirty FROM schema_migrations` returns `true`, and the api boot logs `migrate_failed` and the container crash-loops (the port never binds). **What it means:** for a single `CREATE TABLE` this is unlikely, but if seen, the fix is operational (force the version), not a spec change; the born-verifiable check below asserts `dirty = false` so this does not pass silently.\n- **The failure:** the store methods \"work\" in a unit test with a mock but the real column types or NULL handling differ (e.g. `expires_at` NULL scanned into a non-pointer). **How you'd know:** the integration test against the composed Postgres fails on the NULL round-trip or the type scan, not the mock. **What it means:** E2's verification must run against a real Postgres (the composed db or an equivalent), never only a mock — see Scenarios.\n\n**Anti-pattern:** `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS links`. Why: it makes a double-apply silently succeed, hiding the exact `schema_migrations` bookkeeping bug the plain `CREATE TABLE` plus version tracking is there to catch.\n\n**Anti-pattern:** returning the raw pgx error from `Insert` on a duplicate. Why: it forces every caller (E3) to string-match pgx internals; the typed `ErrDuplicateCode` is the store's contract.\n\n**Anti-pattern:** enforcing or interpreting `expires_at` in E2 (comparing it to `now()`, skipping expired rows). Why: that is E5's TTL slice; doing it here silently pulls E5 forward and breaks the slice boundary.\n\n## 5. Scenarios\n\n```holdout\nGiven a fresh Postgres from `docker compose up -d --build`\nWhen the api container reaches healthy and the startup migration has run\nThen `schema_migrations` contains version 1 with dirty = false\nAnd a `links` table exists whose columns are code (text, PK), url (text, not null), created_at (timestamptz, not null), expires_at (timestamptz, nullable)\n```\n\n```\nGiven the composed stack is up and the migration has applied\nWhen the api container is restarted (`docker compose restart api`) and reaches healthy again\nThen the startup log records the migration runner as \"no change\" (nothing re-applied)\nAnd `schema_migrations` still shows version 1, dirty = false, and the `links` table is unchanged\n```\n\n```\nGiven a running Postgres with the `links` table present\nWhen a Link{Code:\"abc1234\", URL:\"https://example.com/x\", ExpiresAt:nil} is inserted through the store layer and then fetched by code \"abc1234\"\nThen the fetched Link has URL \"https://example.com/x\", a non-zero CreatedAt, and a nil ExpiresAt\n```\n\n```holdout\nGiven a running Postgres with the `links` table present\nWhen ByCode is called through the store layer with a code that was never inserted\nThen it returns ErrLinkNotFound and a zero-value Link\n```\n\n```\nGiven a running Postgres with a link already stored under code \"dup0001\"\nWhen Insert is called again with a Link whose Code is \"dup0001\"\nThen it returns ErrDuplicateCode and the existing row is unchanged\n```\n\n- The migration and `schema_migrations` scenarios are born-verifiable directly against the composed db with SQL (`information_schema.columns`, `SELECT version, dirty FROM schema_migrations`).\n- The store-layer scenarios are verified by the `internal/store` integration test run against a real Postgres (the composed db or an equivalent pointed at by `DATABASE_URL`), never a mock (see the failure mode above).\n\n## 6. Design decision rationale\n\n**How is the `links` table versioned and applied?**\n- Options: a golang-migrate numbered pair applied by the existing E1 runner; a hand-run SQL script; an ORM auto-migrate.\n- The E1 runner already reads `file://migrations` and applies pending `.sql` files at boot; a hand-run script or ORM would duplicate or bypass it.\n- **Chosen:** a `000001_create_links.{up,down}.sql` golang-migrate pair applied by the unchanged E1 runner. This is the seam E1 built for exactly this.\n\n**Where does restart idempotency come from?**\n- Options: rely on golang-migrate's `schema_migrations` version bookkeeping with a plain `CREATE TABLE`; use `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` as a belt-and-braces guard.\n- `IF NOT EXISTS` would mask a double-apply or dirty-state bug; the version row is the real guarantee and is independently checkable.\n- **Chosen:** plain `CREATE TABLE`, idempotency via `schema_migrations`. Restart re-runs the runner, which returns `ErrNoChange`.\n\n**What shape is the persistence API?**\n- Options: a `Links` repository struct with methods; package-level functions taking a pool.\n- E1's OUT OF SCOPE already names a `Links` repository; a struct gives E3/E4 one value to hold.\n- **Chosen:** a `Links` struct via `NewLinks(pool)` with `Insert` and `ByCode`.\n\n**What type is `code`?**\n- Options: `TEXT`, `CHAR(7)`, `VARCHAR(7)`.\n- Fixing a length couples E2 to E3's minting format.\n- **Chosen:** `TEXT PRIMARY KEY`.\n\n**How is a duplicate code surfaced?**\n- Options: a typed `ErrDuplicateCode` sentinel mapped from SQLSTATE 23505; return the raw pgx error and let the caller parse it.\n- The caller (E3) needs to tell a collision from a real failure without string-matching pgx internals.\n- **Chosen:** map SQLSTATE 23505 to `ErrDuplicateCode`. The mapping is a property of a correct insert method, not E3's retry policy (which stays in E3).\n\n## 7. Open questions and assumptions\n\nOpen questions: none. Every decision above is closed; E2 is a mechanical schema-plus-store slice with no open product or architecture question.\n\nAssumptions:\n\n- **Assumes:** golang-migrate v4 and pgx v5 are already project dependencies (they are in `go.mod`: `github.com/golang-migrate/migrate/v4 v4.18.1`, `github.com/jackc/pgx/v5 v5.5.4`). Validation: `grep golang-migrate go.mod && grep jackc/pgx go.mod` before starting.\n- **Assumes:** the Dockerfile copies `migrations/` into the image at `/migrations` (E1 does: `COPY --from=build /src/migrations /migrations`), so a new `.sql` file ships in the api image. Validation: confirm the `COPY ... /migrations` line is present before relying on the compose-level DoD.\n- **Assumes:** the E1 startup sequence calls `migrate.Run(cfg.DatabaseURL, \"migrations\")` before binding the port (it does). Validation: read `cmd/api/main.go` step 3.\n\n## 8. DONE — definition of done\n\n### From WHY\n- [ ] The running service can store and retrieve a code→url mapping: after `docker compose up`, a link inserted through the store layer is readable by code.\n- [ ] `schema_migrations` bookkeeping is populated (E1's deferred point): version 1 is recorded after first boot.\n\n### From WHAT (schema)\n- [ ] A `links` table exists with columns `code TEXT PRIMARY KEY`, `url TEXT NOT NULL`, `created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()`, `expires_at TIMESTAMPTZ NULL`.\n- [ ] The migration is the versioned pair `migrations/000001_create_links.up.sql` and `migrations/000001_create_links.down.sql`; the up file is a plain `CREATE TABLE` (no `IF NOT EXISTS`), the down file drops the table.\n\n### From WHAT (store surface)\n- [ ] `internal/store` exposes a `Link` record with fields `Code string`, `URL string`, `CreatedAt` timestamp, `ExpiresAt` nullable timestamp (nil ⇔ SQL NULL).\n- [ ] `internal/store` exposes `NewLinks(pool) *Links`, `Insert(ctx, Link) error`, `ByCode(ctx, code) (Link, error)`, and the sentinels `ErrLinkNotFound` and `ErrDuplicateCode`.\n- [ ] E1's existing `Open` and `SafeReason` are unchanged.\n\n### From HOW (behaviour)\n- [ ] `Insert` persists `code`, `url`, `expires_at` and lets the DB DEFAULT supply `created_at`; a nil `ExpiresAt` binds SQL NULL.\n- [ ] `Insert` returns `ErrDuplicateCode` on a duplicate primary key (SQLSTATE 23505) and a DSN-free wrapped error otherwise.\n- [ ] `ByCode` returns the populated `Link` on a hit, `ErrLinkNotFound` on no row, and a DSN-free wrapped error otherwise; a NULL `expires_at` reads back as nil.\n- [ ] No query-path error returned by the store contains the DSN.\n\n### From HOW (idempotency)\n- [ ] After an api restart, the migration runner reports \"no change\", `schema_migrations` still shows version 1 with `dirty = false`, and the `links` table is unchanged.\n\n### From HOW (scope boundaries)\n- [ ] E2 adds no HTTP handler and does not modify `cmd/api/main.go` or the router wiring.\n- [ ] E2 applies no expiry/TTL logic: `expires_at` is stored and read back but never compared to `now()`.\n\n### Integration smoke test\n\n```\nPROCEDURE e2_smoke (against the composed Postgres, or DATABASE_URL-pointed test DB):\n  1. docker compose up -d --build; wait for api healthy\n  2. Assert: SELECT version, dirty FROM schema_migrations  →  (1, false)\n  3. Assert: links table has the four columns with the specified types (information_schema.columns)\n  4. Through the store layer: Insert Link{Code:\"smoke01\", URL:\"https://example.com/s\", ExpiresAt:nil}\n  5. Through the store layer: ByCode(\"smoke01\")  →  URL \"https://example.com/s\", CreatedAt non-zero, ExpiresAt nil\n  6. Through the store layer: ByCode(\"nope\")  →  ErrLinkNotFound\n  7. docker compose restart api; wait healthy; assert schema_migrations still (1, false) and step-5 row still readable\n```\n\nconfidence: high\n\n```faff-contract:spec-readiness\n{ \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"decisions\": [ { \"marker\": \"chosen\" }, { \"marker\": \"chosen\" }, { \"marker\": \"chosen\" }, { \"marker\": \"chosen\" }, { \"marker\": \"chosen\" }, { \"marker\": \"assumes\" } ] }\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":489,"cache_read_input_tokens":100972,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":489,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XqTDGk7NCqbki4NjQ15ayY","session_id":"21bdc136-befe-4bc5-9026-5312f63ae86b","uuid":"0c273f48-1b90-4cbf-9c87-9a4753fbccde","timestamp":"2026-09-01T05:04:31.243Z","request_id":"req_011Cec8gDod2PQqUt7VM9JGn","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Prep E2 migration + persistence"}
