{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AiFERmmP7WgmxcTL75Wzi4","type":"tool_result","content":"190\t```\n191\tGiven the composed stack is up and the migration has applied\n192\tWhen the api container is restarted (`docker compose restart api`) and reaches healthy again\n193\tThen the startup log records the migration runner as \"no change\" (nothing re-applied)\n194\tAnd `schema_migrations` still shows version 1, dirty = false, and the `links` table is unchanged\n195\t```\n196\t\n197\t```\n198\tGiven a running Postgres with the `links` table present\n199\tWhen a Link{Code:\"abc1234\", URL:\"https://example.com/x\", ExpiresAt:nil} is inserted through the store layer and then fetched by code \"abc1234\"\n200\tThen the fetched Link has URL \"https://example.com/x\", a non-zero CreatedAt, and a nil ExpiresAt\n201\t```\n202\t\n203\t```holdout\n204\tGiven a running Postgres with the `links` table present\n205\tWhen ByCode is called through the store layer with a code that was never inserted\n206\tThen it returns ErrLinkNotFound and a zero-value Link\n207\t```\n208\t\n209\t```\n210\tGiven a running Postgres with a link already stored under code \"dup0001\"\n211\tWhen Insert is called again with a Link whose Code is \"dup0001\"\n212\tThen it returns ErrDuplicateCode and the existing row is unchanged\n213\t```\n214\t\n215\t- The migration and `schema_migrations` scenarios are born-verifiable directly against the composed db with SQL (`information_schema.columns`, `SELECT version, dirty FROM schema_migrations`).\n216\t- The store-layer scenarios are verified by the `internal/store` integration test run against a real Postgres (the composed db or an equivalent pointed at by `DATABASE_URL`), never a mock (see the failure mode above).\n217\t\n218\t## 6. Design decision rationale\n219\t\n220\t**How is the `links` table versioned and applied?**\n221\t- Options: a golang-migrate numbered pair applied by the existing E1 runner; a hand-run SQL script; an ORM auto-migrate.\n222\t- The E1 runner already reads `file://migrations` and applies pending `.sql` files at boot; a hand-run script or ORM would duplicate or bypass it.\n223\t- **Chosen:** a `000001_create_links.{up,down}.sql` golang-migrate pair applied by the unchanged E1 runner. This is the seam E1 built for exactly this.\n224\t\n225\t**Where does restart idempotency come from?**\n226\t- Options: rely on golang-migrate's `schema_migrations` version bookkeeping with a plain `CREATE TABLE`; use `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` as a belt-and-braces guard.\n227\t- `IF NOT EXISTS` would mask a double-apply or dirty-state bug; the version row is the real guarantee and is independently checkable.\n228\t- **Chosen:** plain `CREATE TABLE`, idempotency via `schema_migrations`. Restart re-runs the runner, which returns `ErrNoChange`.\n229\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XqTDGk7NCqbki4NjQ15ayY","session_id":"21bdc136-befe-4bc5-9026-5312f63ae86b","uuid":"d3934290-55ff-43c2-9657-be4802d688ac","timestamp":"2026-09-01T05:21:19.911Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Prep E2 migration + persistence"}
