{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NV4su8fEJBrABYFiGyvDT4","type":"tool_result","content":"100\t  #   - On no row: returns zero Link, ErrLinkNotFound.\n101\t  #   - On any other DB error: returns zero Link, a wrapped DSN-free error.\n102\t  ByCode(ctx: Context, code: string) -> (Link, error)\n103\t\n104\tSENTINELS:\n105\t  ErrLinkNotFound    # ByCode found no row; E4 maps this to a 404\n106\t  ErrDuplicateCode   # Insert hit a duplicate primary key; E3 maps this to a mint retry\n107\t```\n108\t\n109\t**Insert and `created_at` / `expires_at`.** `Insert` writes `code`, `url`, and `expires_at` and lets the DB default supply `created_at`. It does not read `link.CreatedAt` on the way in. `expires_at` is bound as `NULL` when `link.ExpiresAt` is nil and as the timestamp otherwise.\n110\t\n111\t**Design decision — repository shape vs package funcs.** Options: (a) a `Links` struct holding the pool with methods; (b) package-level functions taking a `*pgxpool.Pool` on each call. E1's own OUT OF SCOPE names \"a `Links` repository in `internal/store`\", and a struct gives E3/E4 a single value to hold and pass to handlers. **Chosen:** a `Links` repository struct constructed by `NewLinks(pool)`. Package funcs would force every future handler to thread the pool through each call.\n112\t\n113\t**Design decision — `code` column type.** Options: `TEXT`, or `CHAR(7)` / `VARCHAR(7)` to match the eventual 7-char code. **Chosen:** `TEXT PRIMARY KEY`. E3 owns the 7-char base62 rule; baking length into the schema here would couple E2 to an E3 decision and force a migration if E3's format ever changed. `TEXT` in Postgres has no performance penalty over `VARCHAR`.\n114\t\n115\t## 4. HOW — behaviour\n116\t\n117\t**Architecture and approach.** E2 touches two seams, both already built by E1:\n118\t\n119\t1. **Migration.** Dropping the `000001_create_links.{up,down}.sql` pair into `migrations/` is the entire startup-side change. `cmd/api/main.go` already calls `migrate.Run(cfg.DatabaseURL, \"migrations\")` at boot; `internal/migrate` already applies pending `.sql` files via golang-migrate's file source and treats `ErrNoChange` as success. With one real file present, the empty-dir short-circuit in `migrate.Run` no longer fires (the dir now contains a `.sql` file), so the runner calls `m.Up()`, which applies version 1 and writes the `schema_migrations` row. No Go code in `main.go` or `internal/migrate` changes.\n120\t\n121\t2. **Store methods.** `internal/store` gains the `Link` record, the `Links` repository, `NewLinks`, `Insert`, `ByCode`, and the two sentinels, using the same pgx v5 pool E1's `Open` already returns. `store.go`'s existing `Open`/`SafeReason` are unchanged.\n122\t\n123\t**Insert behaviour.**\n124\t\n125\t```\n126\tPROCEDURE Insert(ctx, link):\n127\t  1. Execute:\n128\t       INSERT INTO links (code, url, expires_at) VALUES ($1, $2, $3)\n129\t     binding $3 = NULL when link.ExpiresAt is nil, else the timestamp.\n130\t     (created_at is omitted so the column DEFAULT now() supplies it.)\n131\t  2. IF the DB reports unique_violation (SQLSTATE 23505):\n132\t       return ErrDuplicateCode\n133\t  3. IF any other DB error:\n134\t       return a wrapped error whose text carries the DB error, never the DSN\n135\t  4. ELSE return nil\n136\t```\n137\t\n138\t**ByCode behaviour.**\n139\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XqTDGk7NCqbki4NjQ15ayY","session_id":"21bdc136-befe-4bc5-9026-5312f63ae86b","uuid":"a9b40ff5-d8d4-40b5-b6cf-166ceb3e7f9a","timestamp":"2026-09-01T05:43:39.267Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Prep E2 migration + persistence"}
