{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019erutSjvQx2G77kuvNHyeA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# E2 — Schema migration and persistence layer: the `links` table and its store methods\n2\t\n3\t> Spec: faffter-dark-nlspec · 2026-09-01 · autonomous · claude-code/unknown · confidence: high · spec-review: approve.\n4\t> build-tier: complex\n5\t> Spec-review (L4 adversarial, 4 lenses × independent refuters): approve at round 4. Rounds 1-3 were `revise` on QA test-coverage gaps only (architectural, infosec, methodology clear throughout); each round's gaps were closed in place: round 1 — non-nil expires_at round-trip, DSN-free query-error scenario, bounded created_at oracle; round 2 — down-migration correctness, non-empty over-claim corrected, longer/UTF-8 code round-trip; round 3 — IF NOT EXISTS verification, empty-string boundary, airtight created_at (zero-in/non-zero-out) oracle. No objection went unresolved; no scope was expanded to satisfy an E3/E4/E5 concern.\n6\t\n7\tThis is the build spec for epic E2 of the link-shortener service (gitkey `gk-20260901-vesiuq`), written for the build agent and human reviewers. E2 extends the E1 skeleton (gitkey `gk-20260901-r99do5`, shipped and on main): it adds the first real golang-migrate migration, which creates the `links` table, and the persistence-layer methods that insert a link and fetch a link by code, wired into the existing `internal/store` package. E2 inherits E1's fixed stack (Go 1.22 stdlib `net/http`, Postgres 16, pgx v5, golang-migrate v4) unchanged; it decides no new architecture and stands up no new runnable surface. It stops short of the `POST /shorten` and `GET /{code}` HTTP endpoints (E3/E4) and of TTL expiry enforcement (E5).\n8\t\n9\t## 1. WHY — problem and principles\n10\t\n11\tThe load-bearing idea: E1 shipped the migration *runner* against an empty `migrations/` directory, so the schema is still empty and the store package exposes no queries. E2 is the first slice that puts real data structure behind the running service: one migration file that creates the `links` table, and two store methods (insert, fetch-by-code) that read and write rows in it. After E2, the code→url mapping has somewhere to live, which is the precondition every later product endpoint depends on.\n12\t\n13\tProblem statement: the running service has a datastore but no schema and no persistence code, so nothing can be stored or retrieved. E2 adds the `links` table via a versioned migration applied at startup and the store methods to insert and read a link. This resolves E1's deferred point that `schema_migrations` bookkeeping was not yet populated (E1 ran the runner against an empty dir on purpose).\n14\t\n15\tDesign principles:\n16\t\n17\t- **Extend E1, do not re-architect it.** The stack, the migration runner (`internal/migrate`), the connection foundation (`internal/store` `Open`), and the DSN-scrubbing error discipline are fixed by E1. E2 adds a migration file and store methods inside those seams; it changes no existing interface and introduces no new dependency (golang-migrate and pgx are already in `go.mod`).\n18\t- **Idempotency comes from golang-migrate's version bookkeeping, not defensive DDL.** The migration is a plain `CREATE TABLE`, not `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`. Applied-once-ness is guaranteed by the `schema_migrations` version row golang-migrate maintains; restart re-runs the runner, which finds nothing pending (`ErrNoChange`) and makes no change. Defensive `IF NOT EXISTS` would mask a genuine dirty-state or double-apply bug rather than expose it.\n19\t- **The store never leaks the DSN, matching E1.** E1 established that any error which could embed the connection string is scrubbed before logging. E2's query-path errors are wrapped and returned to the caller; they carry the failing SQL and Postgres error, never the DSN, so the query path needs no new scrubbing, but it must never wrap the DSN into an error either.\n20\t- **Persist the `expires_at` column now; enforce nothing on it.** The `expires_at` column is part of E2's schema because the table shape is decided here, but TTL semantics (computing it from `ttl_seconds`, expiry-on-read, expired-code 404) are epic E5. E2 stores whatever `expires_at` a caller supplies (in practice `NULL` until E5) and reads it back unchanged; it applies no expiry logic.\n21\t\n22\tReference context:\n23\t\n24\t| System | Language | Relevance |\n25\t|---|---|---|\n26\t| `internal/store/store.go` | Go | E1 connection foundation (`Open` → `*pgxpool.Pool`, `SafeReason`). E2 adds the `Links` repository and `Link` record here. |\n27\t| `internal/migrate/migrate.go` | Go | E1 migration runner (`Run(dsn, dir)`). Unchanged by E2; it now finds one real migration to apply instead of an empty dir. |\n28\t| `migrations/` | SQL | E1 ships only `.gitkeep`. E2 adds the first `NNNNNN_*.up.sql` / `.down.sql` pair here. The Dockerfile already copies this dir to `/migrations`. |\n29\t| `cmd/api/main.go` | Go | E1 startup sequence. Calls `migrate.Run` then serves. Unchanged by E2. |\n30\t| `docker-compose.yml` | YAML | E1 topology (api + Postgres 16). Unchanged by E2; the born-verifiable checks run against this composed stack. |\n31\t\n32\tScope statement: E2 is the persistence layer of Project P1, sitting between E1 (the runnable skeleton) and E3 (`POST /shorten`, which writes through E2's `Insert`) / E4 (`GET /{code}`, which reads through E2's `ByCode`).\n33\t\n34\t## 2. OUT OF SCOPE\n35\t\n36\t- **`POST /shorten` mint endpoint** — validating a URL, minting a 7-char base62 code, calling `Insert`. Why excluded: epic E3. Extension point: a new handler on `internal/httpapi`'s router that constructs a `Link` and calls `Links.Insert`.\n37\t- **`GET /{code}` redirect and 404** — resolving a code to a URL, issuing a 302, mapping the not-found sentinel to 404. Why excluded: epic E4. Extension point: a wildcard `GET /{code}` handler that calls `Links.ByCode` and maps `ErrLinkNotFound` to a 404.\n38\t- **TTL expiry semantics** — deriving `expires_at` from a `ttl_seconds` input, treating an expired row as absent, returning 404 for an expired code. Why excluded: epic E5. Extension point: E5 computes `expires_at` at mint time and adds an expiry predicate to the read path (`ByCode`, or a new method); E2 only stores and returns the column.\n39\t- **Code generation / uniqueness policy** — deciding how a code is minted or how collisions are retried. Why excluded: epic E3 owns minting. E2's `Insert` surfaces a duplicate-key condition as a typed error so E3 can retry, but it neither generates codes nor decides the retry policy. Extension point: E3's mint loop consumes `ErrDuplicateCode`.\n40\t- **Wiring the repository into an HTTP handler or `main.go`** — constructing `Links` in `main` and passing it to the router. Why excluded: no endpoint consumes it until E3, so wiring it now would be dead code. The repository is exercised by its own integration test against the composed Postgres. Extension point: E3 constructs `NewLinks(pool)` and hands it to the router alongside the existing pool.\n41\t- **Secondary indexes on `links`** — e.g. an index on `expires_at` for an expiry sweep. Why excluded: no E2 query filters on `expires_at`; the primary-key lookup on `code` needs no extra index. Extension point: a later migration under `migrations/` if E5/E6 add an expiry query.\n42\t\n43\t## 3. WHAT — vocabulary, types, and interfaces\n44\t\n45\tVocabulary:\n46\t\n47\t| Term | Definition |\n48\t|---|---|\n49\t| link | A stored mapping from a short `code` to a destination `url`, with a creation timestamp and an optional expiry timestamp. |\n50\t| code | The short identifier that is the primary key of the `links` table. Stored as text; E2 presumes no particular length or alphabet (E3 mints it). |\n51\t| repository | The `Links` type in `internal/store` that owns the SQL for inserting and fetching a link. |\n52\t| `schema_migrations` | The bookkeeping table golang-migrate maintains to record the applied migration version and whether the last migration left the schema dirty. |\n53\t\n54\t### Schema — the `links` table\n55\t\n56\tThe migration creates exactly this table. Column types are Postgres types.\n57\t\n58\t```\n59\tTABLE links:\n60\t  code        TEXT         PRIMARY KEY          # the short code; natural key, lookups are by code\n61\t  url         TEXT         NOT NULL             # destination URL, stored verbatim; no length cap\n62\t  created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ  NOT NULL DEFAULT now()   # row creation time, DB-assigned\n63\t  expires_at  TIMESTAMPTZ  NULL                 # optional expiry; NULL means no expiry. E2 stores, E5 enforces.\n64\t```\n65\t\n66\t- No surrogate key: `code` is the primary key, so a duplicate code insert fails with a unique-violation the store maps to `ErrDuplicateCode`.\n67\t- No `CHECK`, no foreign keys, no secondary indexes in E2.\n68\t\n69\t### Migration files\n70\t\n71\tTwo files under `migrations/`, a golang-migrate versioned pair (6-digit sequential prefix, the golang-migrate default the E1 runner already reads via the file source):\n72\t\n73\t```\n74\tmigrations/000001_create_links.up.sql     # CREATE TABLE links ( ... )   -- plain CREATE, not IF NOT EXISTS\n75\tmigrations/000001_create_links.down.sql   # DROP TABLE IF EXISTS links\n76\t```\n77\t\n78\tThe `up` file creates the table exactly as the schema above. The `down` file drops it. E2 never runs `down` automatically; the pair exists because golang-migrate expects both directions and a reviewer needs the reverse to be correct.\n79\t\n80\t### Types — the store surface (added to `internal/store`)\n81\t\n82\t```\n83\tRECORD Link:\n84\t  Code:       string        # maps to links.code (NOT NULL PK). E2 persists whatever non-null string it is given.\n85\t  URL:        string        # maps to links.url (NOT NULL). E2 persists whatever non-null string it is given.\n86\t  CreatedAt:  Timestamp     # maps to links.created_at; DB-assigned on insert, populated on read\n87\t  ExpiresAt:  Timestamp?    # maps to links.expires_at; nil ⇔ SQL NULL\n88\t\n89\tREPOSITORY Links:\n90\t  # Constructed from the E1 pgxpool. Holds the pool; owns the links SQL.\n91\t  NewLinks(pool: *pgxpool.Pool) -> *Links\n92\t\n93\t  # Insert persists one link row. code is the primary key.\n94\t  #   - On success: returns nil. created_at is DB-assigned (DEFAULT now()).\n95\t  #   - On a duplicate code (unique_violation, SQLSTATE 23505): returns ErrDuplicateCode.\n96\t  #   - On any other DB error: returns a wrapped, DSN-free error.\n97\t  Insert(ctx: Context, link: Link) -> error\n98\t\n99\t  # ByCode fetches one link by its code.\n100\t  #   - On a hit: returns the fully-populated Link, nil.\n101\t  #   - On no row: returns zero Link, ErrLinkNotFound.\n102\t  #   - On any other DB error: returns zero Link, a wrapped DSN-free error.\n103\t  ByCode(ctx: Context, code: string) -> (Link, error)\n104\t\n105\tSENTINELS:\n106\t  ErrLinkNotFound    # ByCode found no row; E4 maps this to a 404\n107\t  ErrDuplicateCode   # Insert hit a duplicate primary key; E3 maps this to a mint retry\n108\t```\n109\t\n110\t**Insert and `created_at` / `expires_at`.** `Insert` writes `code`, `url`, and `expires_at` and lets the DB default supply `created_at`. It does not read `link.CreatedAt` on the way in. `expires_at` is bound as `NULL` when `link.ExpiresAt` is nil and as the timestamp otherwise.\n111\t\n112\t**Validation ownership — E2 persists, it does not validate.** E2's store methods enforce only what the schema enforces: `NOT NULL` on `code` and `url`, and PK uniqueness on `code`. E2 does **not** reject an empty-string code or url, does not validate that `url` is a well-formed absolute URL, and does not constrain the code's length or alphabet — the DB `TEXT` columns accept any non-null string, of any length, in any UTF-8 content. Rejecting an empty or malformed URL and minting a well-formed 7-char base62 code are **E3's** responsibilities (`POST /shorten`); enforcing them in E2 would pull E3's input-validation forward and duplicate it. The `links` table therefore stores exactly what it is handed, and the round-trip scenarios below prove it does so losslessly for varied and long inputs.\n113\t\n114\t**Design decision — repository shape vs package funcs.** Options: (a) a `Links` struct holding the pool with methods; (b) package-level functions taking a `*pgxpool.Pool` on each call. E1's own OUT OF SCOPE names \"a `Links` repository in `internal/store`\", and a struct gives E3/E4 a single value to hold and pass to handlers. **Chosen:** a `Links` repository struct constructed by `NewLinks(pool)`. Package funcs would force every future handler to thread the pool through each call.\n115\t\n116\t**Design decision — `code` column type.** Options: `TEXT`, or `CHAR(7)` / `VARCHAR(7)` to match the eventual 7-char code. **Chosen:** `TEXT PRIMARY KEY`. E3 owns the 7-char base62 rule; baking length into the schema here would couple E2 to an E3 decision and force a migration if E3's format ever changed. `TEXT` in Postgres has no performance penalty over `VARCHAR` and accepts any length and any UTF-8 content, so E2 presumes no code alphabet or length — a longer or non-ASCII code round-trips losslessly (proven by a scenario below).\n117\t\n118\t## 4. HOW — behaviour\n119\t\n120\t**Architecture and approach.** E2 touches two seams, both already built by E1:\n121\t\n122\t1. **Migration.** Dropping the `000001_create_links.{up,down}.sql` pair into `migrations/` is the entire startup-side change. `cmd/api/main.go` already calls `migrate.Run(cfg.DatabaseURL, \"migrations\")` at boot; `internal/migrate` already applies pending `.sql` files via golang-migrate's file source and treats `ErrNoChange` as success. With one real file present, the empty-dir short-circuit in `migrate.Run` no longer fires (the dir now contains a `.sql` file), so the runner calls `m.Up()`, which applies version 1 and writes the `schema_migrations` row. No Go code in `main.go` or `internal/migrate` changes.\n123\t\n124\t2. **Store methods.** `internal/store` gains the `Link` record, the `Links` repository, `NewLinks`, `Insert`, `ByCode`, and the two sentinels, using the same pgx v5 pool E1's `Open` already returns. `store.go`'s existing `Open`/`SafeReason` are unchanged.\n125\t\n126\t**Insert behaviour.**\n127\t\n128\t```\n129\tPROCEDURE Insert(ctx, link):\n130\t  1. Execute:\n131\t       INSERT INTO links (code, url, expires_at) VALUES ($1, $2, $3)\n132\t     binding $3 = NULL when link.ExpiresAt is nil, else the timestamp.\n133\t     (created_at is omitted so the column DEFAULT now() supplies it.)\n134\t  2. IF the DB reports unique_violation (SQLSTATE 23505):\n135\t       return ErrDuplicateCode\n136\t  3. IF any other DB error:\n137\t       return a wrapped error whose text carries the DB error, never the DSN\n138\t  4. ELSE return nil\n139\t```\n140\t\n141\t**ByCode behaviour.**\n142\t\n143\t```\n144\tPROCEDURE ByCode(ctx, code):\n145\t  1. Execute:\n146\t       SELECT code, url, created_at, expires_at FROM links WHERE code = $1\n147\t  2. IF no row (pgx.ErrNoRows):\n148\t       return zero Link, ErrLinkNotFound\n149\t  3. IF any other DB error:\n150\t       return zero Link, a wrapped DSN-free error\n151\t  4. ELSE scan into Link (expires_at → nil when SQL NULL) and return it, nil\n152\t```\n153\t\n154\t**Migration applied-once / restart idempotency.**\n155\t\n156\t```\n157\tPROCEDURE startup_migration (unchanged E1 runner, new file present):\n158\t  1. migrate.Run sees migrations/ is non-empty (a .sql file exists) → skips the empty-dir short-circuit\n159\t  2. Constructs the golang-migrate runner and calls m.Up()\n160\t  3. First boot: version 1 pending → CREATE TABLE links runs; schema_migrations = (version 1, dirty false); status \"applied\"\n161\t  4. Later boot: version 1 already applied → m.Up() returns ErrNoChange → status \"no change\"; no DDL runs, table unchanged\n162\t```\n163\t\n164\tIdempotency on restart is therefore a property of golang-migrate's `schema_migrations` version row, not of the DDL. The `CREATE TABLE` is plain (no `IF NOT EXISTS`): if it ever ran twice against the same schema it would error loudly rather than silently succeed, which is the behaviour that would surface a bookkeeping bug.\n165\t\n166\t**Edge cases and error handling.**\n167\t\n168\t- **Duplicate code on insert** → `ErrDuplicateCode` (mapped from SQLSTATE 23505). Terminal for E2; E3 decides whether to retry with a fresh code. `Insert` distinguishes it from other errors so the caller need not parse pgx error text.\n169\t- **Unknown code on fetch** → `ErrLinkNotFound`. Terminal, expected, not an error condition to log at error level. E4 maps it to 404.\n170\t- **`expires_at` round-trip** → a `NULL` `expires_at` reads back as a nil `ExpiresAt`; a non-nil timestamp reads back equal (to the DB's timestamptz precision). E2 applies no comparison against `now()`.\n171\t- **DB unreachable mid-query** → the pgx call returns a connection error; `Insert`/`ByCode` wrap and return it. It reaches the caller, not the logs directly, and carries no DSN (the DSN lives only in the pool config, not in query-path errors).\n172\t\n173\t**Failure modes.**\n174\t\n175\t- **The failure:** the migration lands the runner in a *dirty* state (a `CREATE TABLE` that partially fails, or a crash mid-apply), after which golang-migrate refuses further migrations until the version is forced clean. **How you'd know:** `SELECT dirty FROM schema_migrations` returns `true`, and the api boot logs `migrate_failed` and the container crash-loops (the port never binds). **What it means:** for a single `CREATE TABLE` this is unlikely, but if seen, the fix is operational (force the version), not a spec change; the born-verifiable check below asserts `dirty = false` so this does not pass silently.\n176\t- **The failure:** the store methods \"work\" in a unit test with a mock but the real column types or NULL handling differ (e.g. `expires_at` NULL scanned into a non-pointer). **How you'd know:** the integration test against the composed Postgres fails on the NULL round-trip or the type scan, not the mock. **What it means:** E2's verification must run against a real Postgres (the composed db or an equivalent), never only a mock — see Scenarios.\n177\t\n178\t**Anti-pattern:** `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS links`. Why: it makes a double-apply silently succeed, hiding the exact `schema_migrations` bookkeeping bug the plain `CREATE TABLE` plus version tracking is there to catch.\n179\t\n180\t**Anti-pattern:** returning the raw pgx error from `Insert` on a duplicate. Why: it forces every caller (E3) to string-match pgx internals; the typed `ErrDuplicateCode` is the store's contract.\n181\t\n182\t**Anti-pattern:** enforcing or interpreting `expires_at` in E2 (comparing it to `now()`, skipping expired rows). Why: that is E5's TTL slice; doing it here silently pulls E5 forward and breaks the slice boundary.\n183\t\n184\t## 5. Scenarios\n185\t\n186\t```holdout\n187\tGiven a fresh Postgres from `docker compose up -d --build`\n188\tWhen the api container reaches healthy and the startup migration has run\n189\tThen `schema_migrations` contains version 1 with dirty = false\n190\tAnd a `links` table exists whose columns are code (text, PK), url (text, not null), created_at (timestamptz, not null), expires_at (timestamptz, nullable)\n191\t```\n192\t\n193\t```\n194\tGiven the composed stack is up and the migration has applied\n195\tWhen the api container is restarted (`docker compose restart api`) and reaches healthy again\n196\tThen the startup log records the migration runner as \"no change\" (nothing re-applied)\n197\tAnd `schema_migrations` still shows version 1, dirty = false, and the `links` table is unchanged\n198\t```\n199\t\n200\t```\n201\tGiven a running Postgres with the `links` table present, and a timestamp t0 captured just before the insert\n202\tWhen a Link{Code:\"abc1234\", URL:\"https://example.com/x\", ExpiresAt:nil, CreatedAt: the zero time (the caller deliberately leaves it unset)} is inserted through the store layer and then fetched by code \"abc1234\", with t1 captured just after the fetch\n203\tThen the fetched Link has URL \"https://example.com/x\" and a nil ExpiresAt\n204\tAnd the fetched CreatedAt is not the zero time and falls within [t0, t1]\n205\tAnd because the inserted Link's CreatedAt was the zero value and `Insert` omits `created_at` from the INSERT column list, a non-zero fetched CreatedAt can only have come from the DB `DEFAULT now()` — an app-assigned or hard-coded value would require Insert to write the column, which this scenario proves it does not\n206\t```\n207\t\n208\t```\n209\tGiven a running Postgres with the `links` table present\n210\tWhen a Link{Code:\"exp0001\", URL:\"https://example.com/e\", ExpiresAt: a specific non-nil timestamp E (e.g. 2030-01-02T03:04:05Z)} is inserted through the store layer and then fetched by code \"exp0001\"\n211\tThen the fetched ExpiresAt is non-nil and equals E to the DB's timestamptz precision (microseconds), with no timezone shift and no truncation\n212\t```\n213\t\n214\t```holdout\n215\tGiven a running Postgres with the `links` table present\n216\tWhen ByCode is called through the store layer with a code that was never inserted\n217\tThen it returns ErrLinkNotFound and a zero-value Link\n218\t```\n219\t\n220\t```\n221\tGiven a running Postgres with a link already stored under code \"dup0001\"\n222\tWhen Insert is called again with a Link whose Code is \"dup0001\"\n223\tThen it returns ErrDuplicateCode and the existing row is unchanged\n224\t```\n225\t\n226\t```\n227\tGiven a running Postgres with the `links` table created by the up migration\n228\tWhen the down migration (`000001_create_links.down.sql`) is applied (e.g. golang-migrate `Down` / a migrate-down test), then the up migration is re-applied\n229\tThen after the down step the `links` table no longer exists (information_schema shows no `links` row), proving the down file is functionally correct and not just present\n230\tAnd after re-applying up, the `links` table exists again with the four specified columns\n231\t```\n232\t\n233\t```\n234\tGiven a running Postgres with the `links` table present\n235\tWhen a Link is inserted through the store layer with a 40-character code \"code-with-a-deliberately-long-identifier-xyz\" (longer than the eventual 7-char format) and a long URL containing a non-ASCII path segment (e.g. \"https://example.com/über/long/path?q=1\"), then fetched by that exact code\n236\tThen the fetched Code and URL are byte-for-byte equal to what was inserted, proving the `TEXT` columns preserve arbitrary length and UTF-8 content with no truncation or alphabet presumption\n237\t```\n238\t\n239\t```\n240\tGiven a running Postgres with the `links` table present\n241\tWhen a Link{Code:\"\", URL:\"\", ExpiresAt:nil} (empty strings, exercising the \"E2 does not validate\" boundary) is inserted through the store layer and then fetched by code \"\"\n242\tThen Insert returns nil (E2 does not reject empty strings — that is E3's validation) and ByCode(\"\") returns a Link with empty Code and empty URL, proving E2 persists rather than validates\n243\t```\n244\t\n245\t```\n246\tGiven a running Postgres with the `links` table already created by the up migration\n247\tWhen the raw `000001_create_links.up.sql` DDL is executed a second time directly against that database (outside golang-migrate's version tracking)\n248\tThen it fails with a \"relation \\\"links\\\" already exists\" error, proving the up file is a plain `CREATE TABLE` and not `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` (a build that used `IF NOT EXISTS` would make this second apply silently succeed)\n249\t```\n250\t\n251\t```holdout\n252\tGiven a running Postgres, and a store method invoked so that a query-path DB error is forced (e.g. Insert against a dropped/renamed `links` table, or ByCode after the table is dropped)\n253\tWhen the returned error's full text (including any wrapped driver error) is inspected\n254\tThen it contains neither the DSN nor the database password in any form (plain, percent-encoded, or path-escaped), matching E1's SafeReason discipline\n255\t```\n256\t\n257\t- The migration and `schema_migrations` scenarios are born-verifiable directly against the composed db with SQL (`information_schema.columns`, `SELECT version, dirty FROM schema_migrations`).\n258\t- The store-layer scenarios (including the non-nil `expires_at` round-trip, the bounded `created_at` oracle, and the DSN-free query-error assertion) are verified by the `internal/store` integration test run against a real Postgres (the composed db or an equivalent pointed at by `DATABASE_URL`), never a mock (see the failure mode above). The DSN-free query-error scenario forces a real query failure (not a connect failure) so the query-path error text is exercised, not merely asserted in prose.\n259\t\n260\t## 6. Design decision rationale\n261\t\n262\t**How is the `links` table versioned and applied?**\n263\t- Options: a golang-migrate numbered pair applied by the existing E1 runner; a hand-run SQL script; an ORM auto-migrate.\n264\t- The E1 runner already reads `file://migrations` and applies pending `.sql` files at boot; a hand-run script or ORM would duplicate or bypass it.\n265\t- **Chosen:** a `000001_create_links.{up,down}.sql` golang-migrate pair applied by the unchanged E1 runner. This is the seam E1 built for exactly this.\n266\t\n267\t**Where does restart idempotency come from?**\n268\t- Options: rely on golang-migrate's `schema_migrations` version bookkeeping with a plain `CREATE TABLE`; use `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` as a belt-and-braces guard.\n269\t- `IF NOT EXISTS` would mask a double-apply or dirty-state bug; the version row is the real guarantee and is independently checkable.\n270\t- **Chosen:** plain `CREATE TABLE`, idempotency via `schema_migrations`. Restart re-runs the runner, which returns `ErrNoChange`.\n271\t\n272\t**What shape is the persistence API?**\n273\t- Options: a `Links` repository struct with methods; package-level functions taking a pool.\n274\t- E1's OUT OF SCOPE already names a `Links` repository; a struct gives E3/E4 one value to hold.\n275\t- **Chosen:** a `Links` struct via `NewLinks(pool)` with `Insert` and `ByCode`.\n276\t\n277\t**What type is `code`?**\n278\t- Options: `TEXT`, `CHAR(7)`, `VARCHAR(7)`.\n279\t- Fixing a length couples E2 to E3's minting format.\n280\t- **Chosen:** `TEXT PRIMARY KEY`.\n281\t\n282\t**How is a duplicate code surfaced?**\n283\t- Options: a typed `ErrDuplicateCode` sentinel mapped from SQLSTATE 23505; return the raw pgx error and let the caller parse it.\n284\t- The caller (E3) needs to tell a collision from a real failure without string-matching pgx internals.\n285\t- **Chosen:** map SQLSTATE 23505 to `ErrDuplicateCode`. The mapping is a property of a correct insert method, not E3's retry policy (which stays in E3).\n286\t\n287\t## 7. Open questions and assumptions\n288\t\n289\tOpen questions: none. Every decision above is closed; E2 is a mechanical schema-plus-store slice with no open product or architecture question.\n290\t\n291\tAssumptions:\n292\t\n293\t- **Assumes:** golang-migrate v4 and pgx v5 are already project dependencies (they are in `go.mod`: `github.com/golang-migrate/migrate/v4 v4.18.1`, `github.com/jackc/pgx/v5 v5.5.4`). Validation: `grep golang-migrate go.mod && grep jackc/pgx go.mod` before starting.\n294\t- **Assumes:** the Dockerfile copies `migrations/` into the image at `/migrations` (E1 does: `COPY --from=build /src/migrations /migrations`), so a new `.sql` file ships in the api image. Validation: confirm the `COPY ... /migrations` line is present before relying on the compose-level DoD.\n295\t- **Assumes:** the E1 startup sequence calls `migrate.Run(cfg.DatabaseURL, \"migrations\")` before binding the port (it does). Validation: read `cmd/api/main.go` step 3.\n296\t\n297\t## 8. DONE — definition of done\n298\t\n299\t### From WHY\n300\t- [ ] The running service can store and retrieve a code→url mapping: after `docker compose up`, a link inserted through the store layer is readable by code.\n301\t- [ ] `schema_migrations` bookkeeping is populated (E1's deferred point): version 1 is recorded after first boot.\n302\t\n303\t### From WHAT (schema)\n304\t- [ ] A `links` table exists with columns `code TEXT PRIMARY KEY`, `url TEXT NOT NULL`, `created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()`, `expires_at TIMESTAMPTZ NULL`.\n305\t- [ ] The migration is the versioned pair `migrations/000001_create_links.up.sql` and `migrations/000001_create_links.down.sql`; the up file is a plain `CREATE TABLE` (no `IF NOT EXISTS`), the down file drops the table.\n306\t- [ ] The down migration is functionally correct, not just present: applying it removes the `links` table (verified by a migrate-down test / golang-migrate `Down`), and re-applying up restores the table.\n307\t- [ ] The up migration uses a plain `CREATE TABLE` (no `IF NOT EXISTS`): executing the raw up DDL a second time against a DB that already has the table errors with \"relation already exists\".\n308\t\n309\t### From WHAT (store surface)\n310\t- [ ] `internal/store` exposes a `Link` record with fields `Code string`, `URL string`, `CreatedAt` timestamp, `ExpiresAt` nullable timestamp (nil ⇔ SQL NULL).\n311\t- [ ] `internal/store` exposes `NewLinks(pool) *Links`, `Insert(ctx, Link) error`, `ByCode(ctx, code) (Link, error)`, and the sentinels `ErrLinkNotFound` and `ErrDuplicateCode`.\n312\t- [ ] E1's existing `Open` and `SafeReason` are unchanged.\n313\t\n314\t### From HOW (behaviour)\n315\t- [ ] `Insert` persists `code`, `url`, `expires_at` and lets the DB DEFAULT supply `created_at`; a nil `ExpiresAt` binds SQL NULL.\n316\t- [ ] `Insert` returns `ErrDuplicateCode` on a duplicate primary key (SQLSTATE 23505) and a DSN-free wrapped error otherwise.\n317\t- [ ] `ByCode` returns the populated `Link` on a hit, `ErrLinkNotFound` on no row, and a DSN-free wrapped error otherwise; a NULL `expires_at` reads back as nil.\n318\t- [ ] A non-nil `expires_at` round-trips through Insert→ByCode equal to the DB's timestamptz precision (microseconds), with no timezone shift and no truncation.\n319\t- [ ] `created_at` is DB-assigned: inserting a Link whose `CreatedAt` is the zero value yields a fetched `CreatedAt` that is non-zero and within the `[just-before-insert, just-after-fetch]` window — proving the value came from `DEFAULT now()` and not the caller (since `Insert` omits `created_at` from the INSERT column list, an app-set value could not appear).\n320\t- [ ] No query-path error returned by the store contains the DSN — verified by forcing a real query failure and asserting the returned error text carries neither the DSN nor the password in plain, percent-encoded, or path-escaped form.\n321\t\n322\t### From HOW (idempotency)\n323\t- [ ] After an api restart, the migration runner reports \"no change\", `schema_migrations` still shows version 1 with `dirty = false`, and the `links` table is unchanged.\n324\t\n325\t### From HOW (scope boundaries)\n326\t- [ ] E2 adds no HTTP handler and does not modify `cmd/api/main.go` or the router wiring.\n327\t- [ ] E2 applies no expiry/TTL logic: `expires_at` is stored and read back but never compared to `now()`.\n328\t- [ ] E2 does no input validation: it persists any non-null `code`/`url` losslessly (any length, UTF-8 content), and does not reject empty strings or malformed URLs — non-empty and URL-format validation, and the 7-char base62 code format, are E3's. A long, non-ASCII code/url round-trips byte-for-byte.\n329\t\n330\t### Integration smoke test\n331\t\n332\t```\n333\tPROCEDURE e2_smoke (against the composed Postgres, or DATABASE_URL-pointed test DB):\n334\t  1. docker compose up -d --build; wait for api healthy\n335\t  2. Assert: SELECT version, dirty FROM schema_migrations  →  (1, false)\n336\t  3. Assert: links table has the four columns with the specified types (information_schema.columns)\n337\t  4. Capture t0 = now(); through the store layer: Insert Link{Code:\"smoke01\", URL:\"https://example.com/s\", ExpiresAt:nil}\n338\t  5. Through the store layer: ByCode(\"smoke01\")  →  URL \"https://example.com/s\", ExpiresAt nil, CreatedAt within [t0, now()]\n339\t  6. Through the store layer: Insert Link{Code:\"smoke02\", URL:\"https://example.com/t\", ExpiresAt: 2030-01-02T03:04:05Z}; ByCode(\"smoke02\")  →  ExpiresAt equals that timestamp (µs precision, no tz shift)\n340\t  7. Through the store layer: ByCode(\"nope\")  →  ErrLinkNotFound\n341\t  8. Force a query error (e.g. ByCode after DROP TABLE links) and assert the returned error text contains no DSN and no password\n342\t  9. docker compose restart api; wait healthy; assert schema_migrations still (1, false) and the step-5 row still readable\n343\t```\n344\t\n345\t## Methodology critique\n346\t\n347\tMethodology: faffter-dark-methodology-agile-delivery\n348\t\n349\t- **Right-sized (principle 4): no issue.** E2 is one migration file plus two store methods (`Insert`, `ByCode`). The schema and the persistence methods always ship together (the table with no methods delivers nothing observable; the methods have no table to hit), so this is a single cohesive 1-2 day unit, not a split candidate. Not too thin to merge either.\n350\t- **Workstream fit (principles 1 + 5): no issue.** E2 sits in Project P1 (\"core shorten-to-redirect on a real datastore\"), an outcome-named container, and carries one outcome — the persistence layer exists. No mixed concerns, no catch-all tickets bundled in.\n351\t- **Surfaced deps (principle 6): no issue.** The roadmap records `E2 blocked-by E1` and `E3 blocked-by E2` explicitly; E1 is shipped and on main, so the blocker is satisfied. The spec's references to E1 code (`internal/store` `Open`, `internal/migrate` `Run`, the Dockerfile `COPY`) all point at merged work, no implicit dep to a ticket that has not shipped.\n352\t- **Risk profile (principle 7): no issue.** E2 is a mechanical schema-plus-CRUD slice on a stack E1 already proved (the golang-migrate runner and the pgx pool both exist and are tested). The one first-time path is the migration runner actually applying a version (E1 only exercised the empty-dir short-circuit), but the `m.Up()` branch already exists in E1's runner, so this is exercise, not novel integration. No de-risking spike warranted.\n353\t\n354\tNo principle violations. Autonomous, high appetite: surface-only for this per-issue critique (no tracker mutation).\n355\t\n356\tconfidence: high\n357\tspec-review: approve\n358\t\n359\t```faff-contract:spec-readiness\n360\t{ \"confidence\": \"high\",\n361\t  \"decisions\": [ { \"marker\": \"chosen\" }, { \"marker\": \"chosen\" }, { \"marker\": \"chosen\" }, { \"marker\": \"chosen\" }, { \"marker\": \"chosen\" }, { \"marker\": \"assumes\" } ] }\n362\t```\n363\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LtKDwPDa79StKedeoJZRSJ","session_id":"21bdc136-befe-4bc5-9026-5312f63ae86b","uuid":"c57d1dea-476d-44f2-b9b6-820ea4245682","timestamp":"2026-09-01T06:36:20.803Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Build E2 migration + store"}
