{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CecKLFnALXrcfuFAQDt2f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The run is complete and closed in a proper terminal state. Here is the final report.\n\n## Beep-boop run complete — `run-20260901-002455-lights-out` (L4 lights-out, git-only)\n\n**Stop reason: product-incomplete** · Duration ~6h55m · Spend 41.4M tokens / $39.95 · 2 build attempts · Sentry: 0 trips · runcheck clean · ground-truth reconcile pass · disposition **needs-attention**.\n\n### What happened\n\n| Epic | Result |\n|---|---|\n| E1 skeleton + compose + `/healthz` | **Shipped** (local merge `da01a8a`) |\n| E2 migration + store layer | **Parked** at the L4 holdout merge floor |\n| E3 POST /shorten, E4 GET /{code}, E5 TTL, E6 errors | **Blocked** by E2's non-merge |\n\n**Section 0a** resolved to `plan` on the greenfield PRD, plotted 6 epics across 2 projects, then fell through to build in the same run.\n\n**E1 is the headline: the full L4 chain worked.** Go 1.22 + Postgres 16 under docker-compose, built through gates + adversarial review, then a genuine **code-blind holdout that hit the running HTTP endpoints** (healthz 200, 404, 405, container health, migration ordering, SIGTERM, non-root uid, DSN-leak scenarios) with **zero needs-human punts**, then merged. That is architecture then env then evaluate proven on a non-faff product.\n\n### The binding constraint (the finding to take back to faff's backlog)\n\nE2 was built correctly (gates green, 11/11 store integration tests against real Postgres, adversarial review pass), but the **code-blind holdout returned needs-human on 2 of 12 criteria and the fail-closed L4 merge floor correctly refused to merge**. I did not override it, and no `met` verdict was fabricated. The two criteria:\n\n1. **Down-migration correctness** — the running stack only runs UP migrations, and a code-blind evaluator has no file access, so this is permanently un-verifiable via the running stack. It is an integration-tier criterion the spec-review QA lens mis-classified into the code-blind holdout DoD.\n2. **Query-path DSN-free error** — E2 exposes no endpoint invoking the store, so it is only verifiable once E3/E4 add the HTTP surface.\n\nRoot cause: spec-review's notion of \"born-verifiable\" (accepts integration-test-level checks) is inconsistent with the code-blind holdout's (running-stack-only). A store-layer epic split from the endpoint epics is too fine-grained for independent code-blind holdout verification. Recommended fix: have `faff dod classify` / the QA lens separate running-stack-verifiable criteria from integration-tier ones, or treat store + first-endpoint as one holdout-verifiable slice at decompose time.\n\n### Secondary findings\n\n- **Adversarial review is the throughput bottleneck.** gemini/gemma (the effective workhorse) 429s under concurrent 4-lens fan-out; spark-qwen (tailnet) intermittently 502s; openrouter/deepseek often returns empty. Roughly 3 to 4 rounds per epic at 10 to 20 minutes each. The backends do work; one subagent falsely reported an outage by improvising a short-deadline probe instead of the prescribed `fan-out.mjs` flow.\n- **Install health is not clean:** 31 faff skills missing from `~/.agents/skills` (run `faff sync` on the host).\n- `.gitignore` does not ignore `.faff/` (worked around locally).\n\nFull detail in `.faff/runs/run-20260901-002455-lights-out/summary.md` and `gk-20260901-vesiuq/park.md`. Exit contract: `faff disposition --run-dir <run>` reads needs-attention.\n\n### To unblock E3 to E6\n\nConfirm the down-migration criterion is integration-test-covered and re-tier it out of E2's code-blind holdout DoD (and hand the query-path-error criterion to E3's holdout), then E2 merges on its 10 met criteria and the chain can proceed. That re-tiering is a human judgment call, which is exactly what the holdout's needs-human is asking for."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usa